window.harnessChunks["rows-6b3e4b0cbcb4ede5.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:47:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jiaxz/open-recoding\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenRecoding</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/jiaxz/open-recoding/tree/4d726930706bd24ff1f99803cf8e1678cae8c61b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 4d726930706bd24ff1f99803cf8e1678cae8c61b\">4d72693 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete coding and investigation work with persistent model-tool actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ambiguous tasks, partial changes, tool errors, incoming peer reports and interruptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose work methods and subsequent tool actions from workspace evidence and communications. - Decision owner: Each bound model actor owns its task decisions. Master and peers may set work assignments and constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-agent context and model binding, continuous loop, mailbox, workspace tools, execution pools and persisted state. - Closure path: Task or peer message → worker model choice → tool observation → revised action and explicit result report or idle continuation. - Boundary reachability: Session starts Master; Master can create an independently running child and deliver its task. The child has its own loop, context, model requests and workspace tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The workers choose and revise their own substantive operations. Runtime transports tools and messages. - Evidence: core/session/session.py, core/multiagent/system.py, core/multiagent/worker.py, core/multiagent/loop.py, docs/sub-agents.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interfering work between independently active agents in a shared workspace and execution pool. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate assignments, competing writes or a wait on another action queued behind the same persistent process. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose file/work ownership, agree on boundaries or change execution routing after conflicts or peer feedback. - Decision owner: Master allocates initial file/work scope and adjusts it. Peers can negotiate local changes directly in their model loops. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role instructions, persistent sender/recipient mail, inbox wake and drain, shared work evidence and revision-aware edit receipts. - Closure path: Shared-file conflict or anticipated overlap → affected worker observes the conflict and exchanges ownership or dependency information with peers/Master → revised assignments or files → later worker action reflects the decision. - Boundary reachability: Default multi-agent role prompts explicitly direct coordination of competing writes and process bottlenecks. Tools route peer communications and reject stale file revisions before writes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the agents leaves conflict detection and message delivery, while the choice of which worker backs off, changes file scope or redirects work requires their discretion. - Distinct S1 units: Persistent worker loops with separate model contexts, tool use and task ownership, operating concurrently in one Session. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Workers can target the same file using stale revisions or queue incompatible work on one shared process. - Attenuating coordination relation: Role-specific instructions direct ownership negotiation and duplicate-work avoidance. Conflict receipts and direct messages let peers and Master select a different work or file allocation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Mail enters the affected model&#39;s next call. A revised file assignment, refreshed file revision or new process allocation changes its next tool action. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The shipped role instructions, conflict receipts and writable shared workspace bind mail to concrete shared-resource interference and subsequent worker behavior. - Evidence: core/multiagent/prompts.py, core/multiagent/prompts.py, core/multiagent/system.py, core/tools/workspacefiles.py, core/session/session.py, docs/sub-agents.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: The reviewed edit tool and coordination path support this response. Effectiveness and consistency across arbitrary raw tools were not measured.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the Session&#39;s current operating commitments and available agent capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled and failed work, resource bottlenecks, duplicated work and changing capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide whether to stop, resume, reassign or transfer current work and process resources. Separately, set a live Session-wide agent capacity constraint. - Decision owner: Master&#39;s model owns discretionary team and process intervention. The human operator owns the live cap decision in the parent path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Global correspondence, roster, pool status, Master-only lifecycle rights, Web operator controls and persisted capacity policy. - Closure path: Master samples current conditions, decides an intervention and invokes the owning tool or sends revised assignments. In the parent path the user updates the cap, the coordinator persists and enforces it, then notifies Master to revise its plan. - Boundary reachability: First-party team prompt and System bind Master tools. Web Session control accepts operator cap changes during operation and calls System.setlimit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Master does more than request a preset timeout. The operator owns cap changes separately, with Master operating inside that returned constraint. - Whole-system current view: Master can access Session agent statuses and cap, correspondence and owners/executions across the shared process pool. The Web operator can inspect the Session-wide roster, occupancy, resource and activity displays. - Current-control decision scope: Master may change assignments, stop/resume agents and preserve or reassign owned process resources. The parent can change the allowed count of concurrently active workers across the whole Session. - Evidence: core/multiagent/prompts.py, core/multiagent/system.py, core/multiagent/system.py, apps/web/recodingweb/host.py, docs/guide.md, docs/sub-agents.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: The parent cap constrains new creation/resume and does not immediately stop an existing worker. Master selects interventions within the current settings.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Inspect worker output and actual failure evidence to challenge an uncertain operational report before choosing corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incomplete or unreliable worker reports, missing artifacts and failed work that left partial files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether and what to inspect independently of the worker&#39;s own message, and judge the actual state of its output. - Decision owner: Master acts as the separate verifier of subordinate claims or partial outputs. Master also owns the subsequent control decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dedicated Master role instructions to validate received and partial artifacts, workspace inspection, host failure and location records, distinct context and Master control tools. - Closure path: Worker report or failure → Master inspects shared artifact and host evidence directly → Master judges completion or defect → resumes, reassigns or explicitly stops the worker and feeds back instructions. - Boundary reachability: Session binds Master prompt and workspace inspection tools. The prompt instructs Master to verify artifacts and, on failure, read actual failure/location and validate partial artifacts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Master can obtain artifact and host evidence without relying on a worker&#39;s self-report. It has an independent context for the audit judgment. - Claim being audited: Whether a reported worker result is supported by the actual artifact or a failed worker left usable work. - Ordinary reporting path: Workers explicitly send progress, result and blocker messages through the normal mailbox/reporting path. - Complementary access path: Master directly reads the artifact or host failure/location and validates the partial work in the shared Session workspace. - Independence boundary: Separate Master model invocation and context relative to the worker under review. Shared workspace and possibly provider; this does not establish independence for Master&#39;s own work. - Who acts on findings: Master chooses whether to correct, resume, reassign or stop, then communicates and applies the choice through first-party controls. - Evidence: core/multiagent/prompts.py, core/multiagent/system.py, core/session/session.py, core/tools/workspacefiles.py, docs/sub-agents.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for distinct access and returned control options; medium for audit selection and effectiveness in operation. - Caveats: Optional model-directed inspection of subordinate work. No mandatory audit pass, independent review of Master itself, or run-level effectiveness is established.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No outside-and-then capability adaptation loop is established within the Session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool availability, runtime limits, package changes and immediate task research needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Anticipate future external changes, select a capability adaptation and return it into present organization. - Decision owner: Workers and Master decide current-task setup. External host operator or coding agent performs maintenance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session packages, context compaction, model configuration, tool inventory and host maintenance utilities. - Closure path: Current-task needs can trigger installing tools or changing local context. An external maintenance action can modify a future deployment. - Boundary reachability: Current work and package operations are supported. The documented host maintenance actor is outside the Session boundary. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current task research, compaction and dependency setup serve production. The first-party Session does not bind a future-environment sensing and capability governance return. - Evidence: core/multiagent/prompts.py, core/session/compaction.py, core/session/packageinventory.py, docs/host-maintenance.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity or ultimate-policy arbitration is established for the assessed Session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Requests to change task scope, worker permissions, runtime resource constraints or external behavior. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a matter that changes the Session&#39;s ultimate mandate and return binding policy into operation. - Decision owner: Human sets purpose and authority in the parent environment. Master manages work and resources inside that scope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Master/sub capability separation, role prompt, task messages, settings and operator controls. - Closure path: Authority limits and task instructions are enforced or interpreted by the current Session. This handles current operations and task direction. - Boundary reachability: The user can message Master and control settings. Master can create, rename and stop workers within the Session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Role identities and discretionary current-control rights do not themselves supply ultimate policy and identity closure. - Evidence: core/multiagent/prompts.py, core/multiagent/tools.py, core/multiagent/system.py, core/session/settings.py, docs/sub-agents.md - Basis: Pinned first-party source and declared distribution. - Confidence: High for the bounded structural finding. - Caveats: Source-static assessment. Provider behavior and real-world quality were not tested.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e68eb43c0e12eb8c1c2b695d64c9c666bba507cb/assessments/op/openrecoding.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e68eb43c0e12eb8c1c2b695d64c9c666bba507cb\">e68eb43 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-13T21:34:38+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/LineLuLan/line-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Line Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-13\">2026-11-13</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/LineLuLan/line-harness/tree/63c98c60b18cfe603381890e104a043627b35ccf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 63c98c60b18cfe603381890e104a043627b35ccf\">63c98c6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute bounded coding or analysis tasks and deliver the resulting changes and evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing repository state, tool results, implementation errors, requirements and review feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose task-local tool actions, implementation and corrections within the packet. - Decision owner: Codex worker in the shipped Claude supervisor/Codex worker assembly. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task packet, dispatch helper, scoped directory, sandbox, session/exit records. - Closure path: Supervisor dispatch → Codex selects actions and edits or analyzes → worker outputs and diff return → corrections can be sent in the existing worker session. - Boundary reachability: Restore installs the supervisor/worker rules and helper. The documented standard path invokes Codex and makes its local output available to Claude. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The worker owns implementation discretion, while the shell and sandbox execute and constrain it. - Evidence: readme, restore, role, dispatch, supervisor. - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: High for the composed source path. - Caveats: The native agent loop is external. The finding is limited to the supported composition.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Separate Codex worker sessions assigned distinct task packets, each responsible for its own repository changes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent edits to the same path in shared development work can destroy or overwrite each worker&#39;s work. - Attenuating coordination relation: The supervisor partitions disjoint paths for parallel work and bundles conflicting scopes into a single worker. A directory lock rejects overlapping live helper dispatches. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The resulting scope and worker assignment are returned as new task packets. A refused dispatch exposes the active run for supervisor follow-up. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The coordination rule is tied to a named cross-worker interference and changes where or when the next worker acts. - Function: Coordinate separately dispatched worker cells to prevent concurrent write interference on shared files. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two packets can target overlapping file paths and overwrite each other&#39;s edits. The shipped operating protocol names this concrete failure mode. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Partition candidate packets by file ownership, allow parallel work when disjoint, and bundle overlapping work into one worker. - Decision owner: Claude supervisor supplied by the installed agent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Worker packet scope, dispatch directory lock, active-run record and supervisor visibility of workers. - Closure path: Supervisor compares packet scopes → partitions or combines overlapping work → dispatches compatible packets → outcomes and blocked or live run information inform the next dispatch. - Boundary reachability: Installed supervisor rules reference the shared delegation protocol and invoke the shipped dispatch helpers. The path-partition choice is given to the agent in normal delegation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The supervisor makes the cross-worker assignment choice. The directory lock enforces a coarser runtime backstop. - Evidence: delegation, supervisor, dispatch, lock. - Basis: explicit operating protocol supported by executable lock and dispatch - Confidence: Medium-high for the documented mode. - Caveats: The lock is per directory. Path-level scope decisions depend on the supervisor, with no separate universal path-conflict oracle.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: For the task team, the supervisor holds all outstanding packet obligations and receives the worker run inventory, current outcome and review status. - Current-control decision scope: Decide which outstanding work is active, whether an attempt is stopped or reconnected, which failed packet is resumed or reissued, and when bounded rework escalates. - Function: Regulate current commitments of the bounded task team by assigning work and intervening in stalled or rejected attempts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent outstanding packets, stale or failed runs, blocked work, invalid diffs and a bounded rework budget. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Use current run state and outstanding packet obligations to continue, reconnect, cancel, revise a packet, or stop and escalate repeated failure. - Decision owner: Claude supervisor acting on the task team as a whole. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run status table, attach/cancel/resume commands, exit codes, review verdicts and human approval boundary for listed actions. - Closure path: Current run states and review evidence reach the supervisor → supervisor selects a continue, cancel, corrective resume or escalation action → helper changes the live or next worker commitment. - Boundary reachability: The installed supervisor instructions wire the helper commands, job statuses and remediation rules into the normal task-team procedure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Job instrumentation reports state and executes intervention. The supervisor decides which commitment or attempt changes. - Evidence: role, delegation, supervisor, status, dispatch, review. - Basis: explicit operating role plus source-level intervention path - Confidence: Medium-high for the bounded task team. - Caveats: This finding does not extend to all projects or to authorizing publication, and assumes normal model adherence to the installed workflow.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: The worker delivered a change that meets the packet&#39;s acceptance criteria. - Ordinary reporting path: The worker returns its completion report and dispatch output to the supervisor. - Complementary access path: The helper reads the actual tracked and untracked Git diff independently of the worker report and supplies that evidence with the packet criteria. - Independence boundary: Fresh reviewer session separate from the implementer transcript; the supervisor independently validates the actual result before handoff. - Who acts on findings: The supervisor returns material findings to the worker for correction or escalates repeated failure. A later review starts fresh. - Function: Inspect a worker&#39;s actual change independently of the worker&#39;s completion report and return corrective findings to the task team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A worker may claim completion despite a deficient diff or unmet acceptance criterion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge the underlying diff against acceptance criteria and, through the supervisor, decide whether rework is needed. - Decision owner: Fresh review model for the supported review tier. The Claude supervisor separately owns the decision to accept or direct remediation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git diff captured by the helper, criteria extracted from the packet, fresh review context, separate supervisor validation and bounded retry. - Closure path: Worker returns outcome → helper obtains independent diff evidence → fresh reviewer issues findings and verdict → supervisor checks the underlying change and routes failure into worker correction or escalation. - Boundary reachability: The review helper is installed by restore and explicitly required for Important-tier work. The packaged supervisor protocol connects a failed review to fresh re-review after correction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Review model owns the evidence judgment. The helper assembles evidence and parses the result, and the supervisor exercises current-control authority over the return. - Evidence: restore, review, diff, verdict, supervisor. - Basis: explicit review contract and source-level artifact access - Confidence: Medium-high for the supported tier. - Caveats: A fresh context can use the same model family. The first-party mode also documents supervisor verification. Lower tiers can omit the fresh model review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No material prospective intelligence and capability decision loop is supplied for the task team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool/library updates, available models, quota and prior review defect rates can affect the team. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Supervisors consult external documentation for current tasks. The weekly helper updates configured packages under a fixed rule, and the owner can revise review-tier policy after a ledger report. - Decision owner: Task agent for current choices, deterministic updater for routine maintenance and human for external changes to operating rules. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Version lookup, config inventory validation and rollback, review ledger, model-selection rules. - Closure path: Docs inform the current packet and version updates keep configured packages current. The review ledger counts internal defects; the inspected paths do not synthesize prospective external options for the team&#39;s capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Dependency refresh and internal quality statistics are distinct from future capability judgment at this recursion. - Evidence: updater, supervisor, review, restore. - Basis: bounded source review - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Weekly updates are documented as machine configuration and the restore script copies the helper. The code shows deterministic maintenance under a preset selection rule.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No operational path adjudicates and returns a change to the task team&#39;s ultimate identity or policy. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Some actions exceed delegated scope and require a human decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The user authorizes a protected action while installed rules define the ordinary supervisor and worker mandates. - Decision owner: Human for protected actions and configuration authors for standing policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission checks, scope instructions, protected-action gates and manual configuration. - Closure path: A human can permit a specific action, while subsequent tasks continue under the existing standing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The rules define and enforce a mandate; the protected-action response resolves a request within it. - Evidence: gates, role, restore, settings. - Basis: bounded source review - Confidence: Medium-high. - Caveats: The human can edit the installed policy externally. No ultimate-policy escalation/return procedure is evidenced in this assessed task-team mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8f9dcce56564cbea84bb114456e4d3f6c0083bbb/assessments/li/line-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8f9dcce56564cbea84bb114456e4d3f6c0083bbb\">8f9dcce &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-11T17:59:51+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/adamentwistle/orchi-build\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Orchi</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-11\">2026-11-11</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/adamentwistle/orchi-build/tree/db5a720ad7cccfd579e26b7ced7a1bbdcf586306\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db5a720ad7cccfd579e26b7ced7a1bbdcf586306\">db5a720 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement and verify bounded software tasks in separate coding-agent work units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving issue requirements, code state, test failures, review findings and tool output. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose edits, testing, fixes and PR contents within a worker brief; the orchestrator also changes briefs after findings. - Decision owner: first-party-launched Claude Code/Codex coding agents operating through Herdr panes or subagent routes; external model inference is not confused with independent Orchi organizational authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktrees, Herdr agent prompt, git/gh CLI, gate commands, worker briefs, pane lifecycle and review notifications. - Closure path: legwork task → orchestrator brief → model worker edits/tests → PR and gate result → review finding or completion → corrective prompt or accepted PR. - Boundary reachability: standard /fanout command dispatches agents in an installed Orchi/Herdr environment using the shipped skill; real third-party agent accounts and dependencies must be configured. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker model chooses implementation and corrective actions; deterministic worktree setup and shell commands carry them. - Evidence: fanout task briefs and dispatch, Herdr agent controls, user-facing commands. - Basis: explicit operational skill and adapter wiring. - Confidence: medium-high for shipped agent mode; no live paid-model confirmation here. - Caveats: real merge rights and approvals remain external; fanout without --merge prepares open review-ready PRs, not auto-merges.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent incompatible concurrent coding work units from producing cross-branch/file conflicts and reconcile their results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping edit scope, PR dependency ordering and newly merged main invalidating sibling branches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose concurrent versus sequential dispatch from task scope and serialize uncertain overlaps, then redirect sibling workers for rebase/conflict repair when main changes. - Decision owner: model-backed project orchestrator executing the first-party /fanout decisions, not just a fixed global semaphore. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: worktree isolation, explicit dependency markers, PR refs, gate reruns, rebase/fix prompts, workerlimit and usage-guard. - Distinct S1 units: independent coding agents assigned to separate PR-sized project tasks/worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: agents can independently change shared files or depend on an ancestor branch that another agent just merged. - Attenuating coordination relation: the planner marks tasks parallel only for disjoint paths/packages and otherwise after &lt;task&gt;; after merges, sibling workers receive a mandatory current-main reconciliation instruction. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: affected workers merge current main, repair conflicts, rerun gates and re-review before their PR can be accepted. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the relation names the actual intersecting-work and stale-branch disturbance and changes worker execution to attenuate it; not merely a mailbox or task list. - Closure path: competing task scopes/merge event → orchestrator serial/parallel or rebase decision → changed worker instructions → new branch/gate result → further adjustment. - Boundary reachability: /fanout runs this decision procedure in its ordinary multi-worker mode; manual single-worker sessions alone would not demonstrate it. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: orchestrator evaluates overlap and remedies from live task/PR facts; scripts and worktrees enforce the chosen route. - Evidence: fanout planning and post-merge sibling handling, Herdr worktree API. - Basis: explicit disturbance and correction path. - Confidence: medium-high structurally; no concurrent PR trace was replayed. - Caveats: actual risk depends on model obedience and repository scope; worker-count cap alone is not the S2 evidence.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current delivery commitments, worker assignments, status and operational bottlenecks for a whole project run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: failed checks/reviews, blocked workers, active PR dependencies, usage cap, outstanding task scope and current resource availability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose work packages, order, routing/model, worker follow-up, blocked/skip/fix outcomes and when work is ready to close. - Decision owner: /fanout orchestrator model within the project; optional switchboard monitors multiple project orchestrators at a higher recursion and relays reserved human choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: legwork fanout-state.md progress board, repo gate, open PR list, bugbot status, worker panes, switchboard status/wait, usage guard. - Whole-system current view: orchestrator loads the full project roadmap and recent log, current open PRs and gate, plus a run-wide worker table with progress/status; switchboard aggregates live project orchestrators and outstanding decisions. - Current-control decision scope: independently plan/dispatch/reprioritize PR-sized tasks, recover blocked workers, fix review findings and preserve handoff state. - Closure path: project state + current PR/agent results → orchestrator scheduling/repair decision → worker dispatch or new brief → status/PR updates → run state and project /wrap. - Boundary reachability: regular /fanout path provides this system-wide state and decision right; switchboard is separate opt-in oversight, not required for the claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model chooses and changes commitments; deterministic health watcher and usage counter cannot make those selections by themselves. - Evidence: fanout load/plan/dispatch/land/cleanup, switchboard state and check-in, README panels. - Basis: structurally wired autonomous present-control mode. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: unconfigured Herdr/Claude accounts and model fallibility limit realized results; no live run audited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: inspect proposed PR code changes separately from worker completion reports and feed correctness concerns into repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: builder mistakes and false confidence despite successful local self-reported tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model reviewer reports real actionable findings or a clean verdict for a specific PR head; orchestrator accepts/rejects findings and orders fixes. - Decision owner: independently invoked Cursor Bugbot when enabled for the repo; otherwise the shipped fallback delegates a code-review pass to Claude Code&#39;s /code-review skill. Provider/reviewer internals are external. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bugbot-wait post-trigger result, GitHub inline comments and review time, code review fallback, repeated tests and three-round bound. - Claim being audited: the coding worker&#39;s PR is correct, safe and review-clean. - Ordinary reporting path: worker&#39;s PR body, completed gate line and self-reported results. - Complementary access path: review service reads submitted PR code/diff and independently reports inline faults; the orchestrator opens flagged file/line and tests fix decisions. - Independence boundary: Bugbot is an external reviewer login distinct from the submitting worker; /code-review fallback is an agent review path but independent vendor/provider identity is not guaranteed. Do not infer an impartial institution from a shared model account. - Who acts on findings: orchestrator/fix worker patches real findings, reruns gate, republishes and requests a new review; unresolved findings block the clean end state. - Closure path: submitted PR → separate reviewer → findings/clean verdict → orchestrator correction and rerun → next review round or blocked result. - Boundary reachability: shipped /pr-review command runs before clean PR is accepted in ordinary /fanout; Bugbot-mode availability depends on GitHub repo setup, fallback is defined for other repos. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: evaluative code findings arise from model reviewer judgment, while bugbot-wait and loops just trigger, convey and enforce the result. - Evidence: PR review command, fanout review/repair loop, bugbot polling tool. - Basis: explicit separate reviewer and corrective loop, optional external integrations. - Confidence: medium for actually independent review, high for wired repair mechanics. - Caveats: model reviewer independence in /code-review fallback is not validated directly; missing review answer times out rather than being considered clean.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party autonomous or parent-closed prospective environment-to-strategy adaptation circuit established for Orchi&#39;s future capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: external tickets, roadmap additions and changing product needs can be consumed as work, but no separate foresight/adaptation decision path is shown. - Decisive decision or feedback right: /fanout decides how to deliver the operator&#39;s existing priorities now; it does not select future positioning or invent an external-environment adaptation program. - Decision owner: user/maintainer may change the legwork roadmap outside the assessed installed harness; no demonstrated S4 actor within it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ticket list, GitHub/Linear adapters, legwork Vision/Next Prompt/Roadmap inputs and task planner. - Closure path: a user-supplied roadmap item enters the current PR task plan; that is S3/S1 task delivery, not yet a first-party prospective S4 circuit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model work decomposition of already-authorized backlog does not alone establish outside-and-then option generation. - Evidence: README ticket sources, fanout load/plan. - Basis: bounded no-path. - Confidence: medium; a configured organization could add separate long-horizon strategy outside this boundary. - Caveats: a future-oriented roadmap is real input but does not by itself prove strategic adaptation ownership.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: preserve the operator&#39;s authoritative project identity and ultimate constraints while delegating ordinary work to coding agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: pressure to execute/merge work contrary to the legwork project&#39;s Vision, human-required credentials, blocked status or reserved outward-facing merge authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: define and revise the project&#39;s Vision and bounded work/merge mandate, including whether an unattended run may merge outward-facing PRs. - Decision owner: legitimate local human project operator who authors the legwork Vision, selects projects/tickets and grants --merge or withholds it; orchestrator cannot unilaterally obtain that merge mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tracker ## Vision, project blockedon, ## Next prompt, worker briefs, default no-merge, switchboard DECISION / ACTION handoff and explicit answers. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: which project the agent team serves and what future work falls inside that mission, distinct from ordinary approval of a single command. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: operator owns project Vision and outward-publication authority; the orchestrator&#39;s task choices are subordinate to that policy in installed /fanout mode. - Return-to-operation path: operator&#39;s tracker Vision/blocked status and merge selection → /fanout reads at startup → task brief, stop condition and merge permission → agent operation/PR outcome; switchboard relays reserved human policy decisions to project orchestrators. - Closure path: local parent updates project Vision/mandate → subsequent run loads it → current worker constraints and publication behavior change, retaining the parent&#39;s authority. - Boundary reachability: shipped /fanout requires a legwork project with Vision and explicitly respects blockedon, human-required steps and default no-merge; its own inputs are available in supported installed mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous orchestrator may select ordinary implementation priorities but not rewrite the operator&#39;s ultimate purpose or grant itself outward publishing rights. - Evidence: fanout project Vision and blocked/merge gates, switchboard decision relays, legwork requirement. - Basis: operational parent-governed local project mission/mandate return path. - Confidence: medium; whether a specific Vision document contains actual identity-level claims depends on deployment, but the first-party format/path is explicit. - Caveats: only local project/operator S5 is claimed, not distributed Orchi OSS governance; ordinary per-task tool approvals are not treated as S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ab0a8f9f8041cf4a2048ddec2fddf17e83e4d7b7/assessments/or/orchi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ab0a8f9f8041cf4a2048ddec2fddf17e83e4d7b7\">ab0a8f9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T13:13:08+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RUC-NLPIR/CoworkOS\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CoworkOS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/RUC-NLPIR/CoworkOS/tree/baf03c30db56160690f9d03bfa7dee925bad6cfd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision baf03c30db56160690f9d03bfa7dee925bad6cfd\">baf03c3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Choose next tools, inspect files/results, perform code/document/visual work and revise a current user task from feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New project requirements, mismatched source files, failed commands/model calls, application render errors and updated user instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model chooses tool actions, task solutions and next revisions after seeing tool outcomes, not just a fixed command list. - Decision owner: Main PiSession model and optional task-specific subagent model loops; pi-agent-core supplies an external generic loop but the shipped composition owns prompts/tools/checkpoints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool capability selection, provider adapter, turn limits, checkpoints, task workspace and headless/desktop run surfaces. - Closure path: User task → model/tool call → first-party tool result or error → resumed model action or explicit final turn. - Boundary reachability: coworkos-app installed CLI/desktop reaches Main sessions with actual registered tool collections. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model makes substantive task and edit choices; the TypeScript session adapter and tool guards enforce constraints. - Evidence: pi-session.ts#L115-L210, run-service.ts#L207-L335, main prompt #L16-L52. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for first-party composed agent/tool feedback. - Caveats: Paid model-provider execution was not reproduced; this is a code-path classification, not a performance result.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Independently running model-backed task subagents with private workspaces, making candidate changes to the same shared document/resource or checking interdependent evidence. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two subagents can base edits on the same older shared resource revision; accepting the stale candidate would overwrite newer changes or invalidate another cell&#39;s work. - Attenuating coordination relation: Scoped read versus candidate grants, private subagent draft, revision compare-and-swap in acceptresourcecandidate, and Main&#39;s explicit re-read/redelegate instruction on collision. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A rejected revisionconflict leaves the current document intact; Main reads the current version, changes the affected worker&#39;s scope and re-delegates a new candidate. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The version-conflict witness, exclusion of stale writes and returned Main-to-worker corrective plan directly attenuate an identified shared-artifact interference between parallel operational cells. - Function: Regulate cross-worker edit collisions and mismatched artifact dependencies while preserving independent task execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Revision races, overlapping subagent edits, private candidate conflicts and stale evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select whether to accept a candidate, which changed resource to inspect and how to reassign conflicting work. - Decision owner: Model-driven Main, instructed in first-party standard prompt to check and re-delegate; deterministic resource store enforces revision safety. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Resource candidate IDs/baseRevision, transactional acceptance, explicit scoped tools, peer message and durable callbacks. - Closure path: Worker candidate → Main inspection → atomic acceptance or revision conflict → latest resource read → new Subagent instruction/candidate. - Boundary reachability: App-scoped candidate mode and the acceptresourcecandidate tool are exposed in the standard application/CLI composition, when the user works with supported shared resources. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Main model decides the corrective coordination response. Compare-and-swap only prevents an unsafe stale write; it does not select the next division of work. - Evidence: main prompt #L39-L51, application-bridge.ts#L829-L875, resource-store.ts#L450-L490. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for app-resource candidate mode; generic filesystem editing alone has no such protected S2 path. - Caveats: The effective coordination actor is Main; no self-organizing peer conflict negotiation is assumed from sendpeermessage.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Main can query per-run total/running/failed/completed task counts and inspect each subagent&#39;s tool traces, diagnostics, deliverables, pending callbacks and active questions through first-party status/wait tools. - Current-control decision scope: Decide the number/scope/timing of subagent tasks, which run to cancel or steer, which candidate to adopt, whether to reallocate work after a failure and when current task commitments are complete. - Function: Manage current subagent portfolio and accountable integration of ongoing project work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel bottlenecks, stalled or redundant workers, wrong deliverables, missing evidence, unexpected cancellations and unsatisfied task completion criteria. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Start/stop/steer bounded worker assignments and accept/reject completed work after whole-run progress and artifact evidence. - Decision owner: Main model in the supported delegated-task mode; deterministic coordinator, status tool, queues, human UI and resources execute or constrain those decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: getsubagentstatus, waitforsubagents, sendsubagentmessage, cancelsubagent, durable scheduler, artifact boundaries and model Main acceptance. - Closure path: Whole-run status/callback/artifact feedback → Main resource and priority decision → reassign/steer/cancel/accept workers → changed subsequent work and verified final output. - Boundary reachability: Main prompt and standard coordination tools expose these rights in the installed CLI/Desktop task flow. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model Main selects current intervention based on a cross-worker view; deterministic lease priorities alone would not establish autonomous S3. - Evidence: progress.ts#L49-L165, coordination.ts#L345-L535, main prompt #L39-L52. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high when subagents are actually used. - Caveats: User cancel/steer controls exist as ordinary intervention; a distinct complete parent-governed S3 mode is not inferred solely from UI buttons.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No sufficiently separate complementary-audit decision/feedback channel established apart from ordinary Main acceptance of worker artifacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect worker claims and faulty artifacts are relevant, but task-level verification/inspection is assigned to the same Main that owns daily integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently sample actual operational reality, challenge the normal reporting path and return an auditor&#39;s finding to accountable correction; no shipped dedicated assessor closes that role. - Decision owner: Main can inspect raw candidate files and optionally delegate independent fact-checking; there is no distinct mandated or condition-triggered auditing actor in the base system. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: browseworkspace, candidate read, source refs, test outputs, revisioned artifact review, optional independent Subagent task. - Closure path: Main reviews ordinary work and accepts/reassigns; no established independent findings channel bypassing or checking Main&#39;s own standard report. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A truthful worker result check and the ability to call a generic reviewer do not alone establish complementary S3 ownership. - Evidence: main prompt #L43-L52, progress.ts#L55-L85, application-bridge.ts#L829-L875. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; optional high-risk independent-review composition remains available. - Caveats: A deployment with an explicitly independent auditor could change the result; it is not credited without that first-party standard mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: Watchers poll externally changing research/web/file sources, or rule-packaged conditions and user-defined long-term goal events; those new observations can differ from the Main&#39;s last task snapshot. - Future / prospective distinction: Durable goal plans name a next review time, waiting condition and prospective next action, and a later event/review prompts Main to reconsider the plan rather than merely repeat the current coding turn. - Adaptation option generated: Main model changes monitoring sources/thresholds, future goal plan or next action, requests human judgment when needed, and optionally revises persistent watcher definitions. - Path back into current capability / S3: GoalSupervisor/WatchSupervisor deliver revisioned external events to Main Inbox, Main changes the stored goal&#39;s plan/monitoring and dispatches subsequent subagent or local work accordingly. - Function: Sustain prospective model-driven adaptation of a standing goal in response to future environmental evidence and time. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving outside information, missing/expired monitoring, long-idle plans, new evidence and goals that need human escalation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a revised goal strategy, watch conditions and work initiation after an external change is observed. - Decision owner: Main model when managed-goal/virtual-agent monitoring is activated; deterministic watchers handle polling/idempotent wake and human answers can constrain a particular adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Durable GoalStore, managegoal/publishgoalupdate, WatchSupervisor, GoalSupervisor, nextReviewAt, pending external events and monitor state. - Closure path: Environmental trigger or future review tick → durable Inbox wake → Main assesses outside change → stored goal/monitor adaptation → renewed current work/next review. - Boundary reachability: Documented standing-goal mode exists in the shipped CLI/Desktop tools, but requires explicit goal and watcher activation, not automatic creation from ordinary task wording. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The watcher does not choose strategy; Main model judges the changing situation and returns revised future action into its work portfolio. - Evidence: goal-supervisor.ts#L13-L115, watch-supervisor.ts#L95-L165, goals.ts#L130-L205. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for conditional monitored-goal mode. - Caveats: Paper-reported RSI training/evaluation gains are not reproduced or imported as runtime S4. A no-watcher ordinary session does not automatically retain prospective capability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party organization-level ultimate-policy issue with an authoritative ruling returned into current project governance is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unsafe tools, permission mistakes and unknown user requirements are handled through ordinary access controls or task-local askhuman. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide a binding change to the project&#39;s ultimate identity/constitutional policy after a legitimate appeal; no such path is wired. - Decision owner: User chooses goals/tool grants, Main chooses task actions, deterministic runtime enforces access and completion contracts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role prompt, capability grants, app-resource scopes, human decisions and optional watch policy/notification settings. - Closure path: Tool/user clarification constrains current work; no identity-level proposal → ultimate authority → changed governance for subsequent runs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A named Main persona, tool permission and human request are not evidence of autonomous or legitimate-parent S5 by themselves. - Evidence: agent-identity.ts, task-capabilities.ts, goal-management.ts#L230-L355. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Maintainer governance of the OSS project is a separate recursion and is not automatically transferred into a user&#39;s runtime task.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6ff03367116370b4f39d04f3b9737febf29b0de3/assessments/co/coworkos.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6ff03367116370b4f39d04f3b9737febf29b0de3\">6ff0336 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:56:04+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/WeiminWu2000/Multi-Agent-Harness-2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Multi-Agent Harness²</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/WeiminWu2000/Multi-Agent-Harness-2/tree/1ac2ad1c2793805c72623a6086bb80c91063c532\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1ac2ad1c2793805c72623a6086bb80c91063c532\">1ac2ad1 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: agent roles perform public-environment task work and turn observations into actions and final deliverables. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing AppWorld task requirements, API responses, intermediate state changes, execution errors and incomplete task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: analyzer chooses inspection code; planner decides next work; coder/debugger choose executable API actions; finalizer chooses submitted task response. - Decision owner: model-driven role actors instantiated by Team.ask; Team.run delivers their choices to World.execute and subsequent role prompts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded Gateway.request, AppWorld worker, maxteamsteps, typed role JSON and persistent trace/event store. - Closure path: task/public API evidence → role model decisions → Python API effects/results → shared state/public interaction history → subsequent model decision and finalizer. - Boundary reachability: mah2 appworld --method harness2 --execute runs the packaged first-party role actor with configured provider; base runs the same team without improvement; neither depends on an unshipped custom executor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model roles select substantive next operations; AppWorld and gateway execute/limit, rather than autonomously deciding task content. - Evidence: team, AppWorld worker, README CLI. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no credentials were supplied for a live paid provider/AppWorld run; code wiring and supported launch mode, not achieved benchmark success, are assessed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: analyzer, planner, coder, verifier, router, debugger and finalizer each make model-directed role choices over the same task within Team.run. - Inter-S1 disturbance: coder may act on stale/incomplete planner context; verifier&#39;s corrective diagnosis can be lost or ignored by router/planner; mismatched producer/consumer handoffs can perpetuate failed work cycles. - Attenuating coordination relation: proposalb model editor targets one diagnosed handoff or feedback route and its producer/consumer contract, probes the change in a separate team run, and the composer/selector adopts a tested version. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: selected coordination routes and contracts alter future Team.ask handoff fields and instructions; the next round runs the selected harness rather than a mere logged proposal. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the editor identifies an actual role-interference mechanism from event evidence and changes both ends of a concrete coordination contract; a generic mailbox or static ordered role call would not do so. - Function: diagnostic handoff repair regulates erroneous or lost information between planning, coding, verification, routing and later corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stale/omitted verifier diagnosis, mismatched producer-consumer requirements, coder missing task context and repeated failed plan/execution handoffs among distinct role S1 cells. - Decisive decision or feedback right: propose or reject a one-route coordination change from actual public execution events and apply selected shared contracts/diagnostic routes to later role interactions. - Decision owner: independent scoped model proposalb chooses targeted coordination repairs, model composer elects between validated coordination options based on candidate traces; deterministic schema checker limits legal edits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coordination.version, contracts, plannerreceivesdiagnosis, routerreceivesdiagnosis, verifierdiagnosis, paired sender/receiver instructions, schema-scoped edits and fresh candidate probes. - Closure path: execution trace of analyzer/planner/coder/verifier/router interdependence → coordination editor diagnoses lost/ignored information → candidate probe/composer selection → chosen harness&#39;s revised contracts/diagnosis reach later Team.ask handoffs and change subsequent S1 behavior. - Boundary reachability: AppWorld harness2 directly executes the coordination editor and applies selected harness[&#39;coordination&#39;] in the bundled Team.ask, with carried-forward harness in the next round; this is not only the generic custom-team callback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model editors choose whether/which interference to attenuate, while runtime validates schemas and transports the chosen handoff; generic role sequencing by itself would not establish this S2 claim. - Evidence: coordination editor, team handoff, coordination schema, architecture. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: fixed base mode may not enable diagnosis routes; the positive is from documented operational harness2 adaptation and composition, not from a mailbox/edge alone. Actual realized coordination improvement is unproven without a provider run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: the router receives the team&#39;s current plan and execution feedback, optional verifier diagnosis and bounded public interaction history across the seven-role task unit. - Current-control decision scope: accept/finalize the team&#39;s current work or commit remaining execution steps to another planning/coding cycle; error events can route current resources to debugger recovery. - Function: current task-team progress supervision decides whether to continue work or seek final completion, with bounded present-run intervention on errors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished task state, coding errors, contradictory/incomplete verification and scarce team execution steps. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select continue versus finalize based on team execution state and verifier diagnosis; select whether finalizer proceeds or planner/coder receives another work cycle. - Decision owner: model-directed router role, supported by the verifier judgment and deterministic Team.run scheduling. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: team state (plans, execution, verification, routing), step cap, JSON route parsing, worker lifecycle and latest public history. - Closure path: team execution/verification observations → router model decision → Team.run branches to finalizer or next planner/coder cycle; failed code triggers debugger branch for team recovery. - Boundary reachability: router, verifier and conditional finalizer/next-plan branches run as part of the packaged AppWorld task team with no separate unshipped manager plugin. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: router owns a task-team-level current commitment and intervention decision; static loop limits enforce developer budgets but do not choose the team route. - Evidence: Team.run, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: supervisory scope is the one-task team, not cross-task organization/resource governance. This is more than speaker selection only insofar as the route controls the team&#39;s present execution commitment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: sufficiently independent complementary observation and challenge of operational claims is not established inside the shipped runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect execution assumptions and incomplete progress are discussed by verifier, while official task correctness is independently checked only after final commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect operational reality via a separate enough evidence route, challenge ordinary reports and return corrective findings into team control. - Decision owner: verifier model critiques the same public interaction history and handoff state.execution it receives from coder/runtime; official AppWorld grader is a separate post-commit evaluation dependency, not in-run auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier role prompt, diagnostics in shared state, trace and optional official grader after selection. - Closure path: verifier can return a progress diagnosis into router/planner, but lacks a distinct complementary source-observing evidence acquisition path. Official grading occurs only after selection, never returns into live candidate choices. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model called verifier is a routine in-band checker, and post-commit external benchmark evaluation cannot be treated as a first-party S3 owner. - Evidence: Team.ask/Team.run, post-commit grader boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: verifier has distinct role prompt and reasoning, but no evidenced independent operational data path distinct from the normal team reports; an added self-check tool could change this result at another pinned revision.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - External distinction: observed AppWorld public API responses, task demands, failure traces and execution outcomes reveal environmental requirements rather than merely internal source-code intentions. - Future / prospective distinction: diagnosis targets the next execution of the team, via alternate role/coordination instructions in new candidate worlds and the subsequent outer round, not only repairing the current API call. - Adaptation option generated: model role and coordination editors propose bounded harness variants; composer selects/revises a successor based on independent candidate probes and observed public evidence. - Path back into current capability / S3: current-round selector commits selectedharness.json; runrecursive uses that configuration as the next reference team, changing role and handoff capability while the router retains present execution control. - Function: adapt future team operating capability to observed public task/environment failures and potential coordination changes rather than merely repairing the current line of code. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatch between role procedures/handoffs and evolving public task requirements, recurring mistakes, and unsuitable coordination for subsequent rounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose evidence-linked role/procedure options, test alternatives, compose a successor harness and choose the next-round configuration. - Decision owner: model-directed proposala and proposalb editors, composer and selector, with the recursive controller transporting the selected harness into the next round. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: trace event IDs/citation validation, candidate isolation, stage budgets, scope-limited schema validation, current-round selection, lesson library and program/harness identity checks. - Closure path: reference task/environment trace → role and coordination model hypotheses → fresh candidate probes and composition → public-evidence selector commitment → runrecursive carries selectedharness.json forward as the next round&#39;s operative role/coordination capability. - Boundary reachability: bundled AppWorld harness2 mode has two configured rounds and invokes these first-party editors/selector/recursion paths; not only an unshipped development benchmark or an external research paper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LLM stages choose change proposals and selections; budget checks, schema constraints and trace citations enforce without making those adaptation judgments. - Evidence: AppWorld optimizer, round execution, recursive carry, architecture. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this is prospective task-local next-round adaptation only; no claim of persistent fleet-wide intelligence or cross-task self-improvement, and no successful improvement is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no ultimate identity/policy decision loop over team/optimizer is shipped. - Disturbance / variety regulated: API budgets, editable roles/allowed coordination routes and official evaluation separation are bounded by static developer/operator policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide/revise team/system ultimate policy or identity and authoritatively return it to later operations. - Decision owner: external developers/operator choose hard-coded schema, model/budget/environment and command flags; model editors may change role procedures and existing bounded routes but cannot change identities, roster, tool infrastructure, budget, evaluator or ultimate policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: schema validate, restricted editor surfaces, stage budgets, protected grader separation and configuration file. - Closure path: invalid schema/policy changes are rejected; task-local harness procedure updates are S2/S4-level capability changes, not ultimate-policy decisions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a configured governance limit, role prompt or operator selecting --grade does not itself provide an identity-level issue→decision→returned authority path. - Evidence: schema constraints, editor constraints, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: model-proposed coordination rewrites do not promote routine task policy to S5, and external maintainer roadmap is outside this runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ad85298fcc3e4198279347f7151423777b8d57d3/assessments/mu/multi-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ad85298fcc3e4198279347f7151423777b8d57d3\">ad85298 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-05T17:15:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/taewooyo/tram\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tram</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-05\">2026-11-05</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/taewooyo/tram/tree/cf676742728b4d85020b210c8f0e6aa922f51fd0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision cf676742728b4d85020b210c8f0e6aa922f51fd0\">cf67674 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: main or separately dispatched coding agents investigate a specific project, change implementation code/tests and return concrete source-level results; reviewer roles additionally perform verification work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code and dependency behavior, changing requirements within approved scope, tool/test failures and reviewer-discovered defects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose source modifications, affected tests, debugging actions and fixes using actual project context while the user-approved PRD/Plan bounds the task. - Decision owner: model using the Tram role contract on an installed Codex/Claude host; the Python runtime validates allowed phase transitions but does not author code. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party role instructions, project context cards, scoped worker dispatch, bounded implement/review transition, task evidence records and verify-run assertions. - Closure path: user-approved task and plan → model investigates files and implements/tests → independent verification returns cited defects → implementing model revises work → passing head-specific evidence permits completion. - Boundary reachability: package exposes the documented /tram:start and implementation roles; its skill contracts explicitly dispatch tool-using workers through host-native adapters and connect their results to installed runtime transitions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: source-change choice and correction require meaningful task-specific model discretion. The host and model supply inference, but Tram&#39;s first-party skill construction makes the role operationally reachable; Python gates remain constructor enforcement. - Evidence: main dispatcher and per-round work, start continuation, release verification limits. - Basis: documented packaged skill/host role wiring plus shipped transition implementation, without provider-backed full workflow acceptance. - Confidence: medium-high for supported construction, medium for untested integrated host lifecycle. - Caveats: no independently observed authenticated full run was performed; installed host/provider tools are required and a mere package-install success does not prove agent edits work end to end.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: main model decides which concurrently eligible operational sub-tasks are genuinely independent and serializes those competing for files, ports, databases or outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: separate implementer agents overwriting a shared file, racing on a DB/port/output directory or acting on an unfinished prerequisite. - Decisive decision or feedback right: identify each task&#39;s owned files/resources/dependencies, select parallel dispatch only for disjoint units, and order shared-write or dependent work sequentially; inspect active/aborted worker state before reassignment. - Decision owner: the model-based main orchestration role applying a mandatory first-party coordination contract; worker agents cannot re-delegate or rewrite the common Task/approval state. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Task entries with owners/paths/dependencies, adapter-provided independent agent invocation, one main Task writer, active-worker liveness check and shared-resource serial-first policy. - Closure path: proposed worker assignments → main compares file/port/DB/output ownership and prior completion → independent tasks may run concurrently, but shared resources wait → main reads workers&#39; actual edits/results → updates Task and only then authorizes dependent work. - Boundary reachability: the single-orchestration contract expressly governs all skill paths, including impl, quick, coverage and perf; implementation dispatcher links native agent delegation back to main Task reconciliation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the coordination rule sets constraints, but the model must classify live task/resource overlap and exercise discretion over serial versus parallel work; these are cross-agent interference decisions, not a generic speaker selector or mere worker naming. - Distinct S1 units: multiple separately spawned implementer agents with independently owned sub-tasks in one approved project plan. - Inter-S1 disturbance: conflicting writes to a shared file or mutable runtime resource can invalidate another worker&#39;s results. - Attenuating coordination relation: main-assigned ownership and dependency records; only independent resource sets are dispatched concurrently, while shared operations are serialized. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a dependent/shared-path worker is withheld until prerequisite work has returned and current state is reread, then receives revised files/instructions rather than writing concurrently. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the decisive discriminator is the specific material collision of two operational workers, and the chosen serial/parallel pattern directly prevents that collision. - Evidence: resource-conflict dispatch, implementation dispatch choices, ownership and progress state. - Basis: explicit model-directed first-party contract and persistent task ownership, not observed measured parallel conflict traces. - Confidence: medium; the prevention is prompt/protocol-directed rather than an atomic cross-process file reservation. - Caveats: a worker can violate its assigned ownership and no universal OS-level lock is claimed across all worker-edited files; runtime write lock protects metadata, not all project source.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: model-based main maintains a whole-task view across worker assignments, completion evidence, verification rounds, resource/budget/approval limits and active blockers while a human controls the current task envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatched PRD and implementation scope, missing worker results, stale check evidence, repeated failed reviews, excessive rounds and unreliable execution state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: main assigns/changes worker tasks, chooses direct versus delegated execution, selects review strength, integrates outcomes and branches to fix, complete or design escalation; human separately ratifies PRD/Plan and may halt/change permitted work. - Decision owner: operating main model for current work allocation and stage strategy (A), constrained by the user as parent authority for approvals/current commitments (P). Python refuses invalid transitions and unverifiable references. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project Task board, status/next-action state, scoped worker references, digest-bound approvals, round budget, 90-point full-review min-gate and transition authority. - Closure path: current tasks and worker results → main chooses new assignment/verification route or escalates → runtime validates evidence and updates state → next worker/reviewer receives changed instructions or waits for human approval. - Boundary reachability: start routes to impl/verify and main owns transition, approve and review in the documented packaged host workflow; tests implement the refusal boundaries. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model has real discretion in prioritizing and integrating this task&#39;s workers, although it cannot change approved scope or bypass explicit owner gates. - Whole-system current view: main reads complete per-task stage, Task worker owners/dependencies, verification status and blockers, rather than only one code-edit output. - Current-control decision scope: division and ordering of sub-tasks, live delegated work, verification effort, retry/stop/escalation and current human authorization envelope. - Evidence: main/worker authority split, implement/review branching, state/approval implementation. - Basis: first-party main-role/worker stage contract plus built runtime validation. - Confidence: medium-high for control boundaries, with integrated host execution unverified. - Caveats: the recursion is a bounded development task, not evidence of a standing multi-project autonomous executive or direct PR merge authority.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a new isolated reviewer agent independently checks the current implementation&#39;s real tests/code and user-approved acceptance criteria against the implementer&#39;s report. - Disturbance / variety regulated: untested or mis-scoped changes, stale evidence after HEAD edits, fabricated test success, missing project policy and review contamination from earlier rounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: fresh reviewer performs its own verify-run, checks diff and tests against PRD/Plan, assigns five-axis substantiated verdict and either passes or cites blocking defects; main must validate recorded independent execution and reject contradictory results. - Decision owner: an independently spawned model reviewer with a separate run/reference and strict no-implementation role; main registers but does not replace its independent inspection. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verify full mandatory fresh reviewer, 5-axis rubric, verify-run command receipts, start snapshot/round check, review registration and refusal of missing/invalid evidence. - Closure path: implementation change → new reviewer independently reads current code/tests and runs checks → evidence/score/verdict returned → main validates &amp; stores reviewed round → failed defects go to implementer fix; passed verified round allows completion. - Boundary reachability: start/impl require full review for new/high-risk work, and verify explicitly routes to the host&#39;s native isolated agent creation and runtime review registration; if independent review is unavailable the project must withhold completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an independent role makes qualitative adversarial judgment about the observed diff and actual tests; mandatory runtime scoring checks only enforce evidence validity. - Claim being audited: implementation meets the approved PRD/Plan and passes required tests at the current state. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer explanation, local test claims, Task progress and completion summary. - Complementary access path: fresh separate reviewer&#39;s own tools reading the source diff/test outputs plus original user-intent provenance and approved criteria, independent of implementer&#39;s prior conversation. - Independence boundary: new run, separate reviewer role/context and no implementation edits; shares the configured provider/model infrastructure and is not an external institution. - Who acts on findings: main controller rejects completion and routes concrete cited defects to another implementation round; changed code requires a new reviewer. - Evidence: full independent verifier, rubric and evidence, main round branch. - Basis: mandatory packaged role workflow and hard evidence checks, not a claimed completed public host review run. - Confidence: medium-high for separation contract, medium for unobserved fidelity in a full authenticated session. - Caveats: the release notes do not establish a full real Codex/Claude review cycle on the candidate package, and the same model provider may supply implementer and reviewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate operational loop that models changing external/future project conditions, develops adaptation options and returns a selected enduring capability to the current task organization was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving third-party requirements, dependencies, model/tool capabilities and future market needs could motivate a different project strategy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: project research/domain skills can gather context; the human approves policy and scope, but no prospective outside-environment option-and-return authority was observed within the installed organization. - Decision owner: not established at S4. Main researches the current approved task; user/maintainers may later choose new project scope outside that loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: code research, project knowledge, existing-doc evidence, domain interviews, retrospectives, and performance experiments. - Closure path: observed project facts influence current PRD/Plan and code work; no separate future-external scenario selection is shown governing an enduring adapted operational capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: planning present implementation, repeating tests or gathering knowledge is not by itself the outside-and-then S4 relation. - Evidence: project knowledge bounds, start/approved work scope, workflow limits. - Basis: bounded negative examination of shipped task, knowledge and improvement workflows. - Confidence: medium-high at the per-project task recursion. - Caveats: a user&#39;s own future product planning might provide S4 in a larger organization, but is outside this installed task-level scope.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no installed organizational identity-level agenda, legitimate decision on ultimate policy and binding charter-return loop was established within this bounded task workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a fundamental change to the project&#39;s purpose, ethics or ultimate product authority could exceed a scoped coding assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human must approve PRD/Plan and policy/requirements changes, but the shipped approvals are for a task&#39;s scope/design, not shown as a continuing VSM S5 identity function. - Decision owner: human project owner retains ultimate responsibility outside Tram&#39;s implemented local task orchestration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: explicit requirement/design approvals, immutable snapshots, scope conflict escalation, project config and user-controlled publication. - Closure path: new requirements or product/security policy choices are escalated to human PRD/Plan review and return to this task; no evidence such a decision returns as binding revised organization identity across recurring tasks. - Boundary reachability: documented owner approvals and repeated validity checks are real; an identity/policy-level recursion beyond them is not. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: important parent approval does not alone establish S5(P) where the closed issue is task scope, not ultimate mission/ethos or constitutional authority. - Evidence: approval/change handling, human decision boundaries, workflow gate rules. - Basis: first-party installed flow source review, distinguishing task approvals from ultimate organizational policy. - Confidence: medium-high within this scope. - Caveats: an enclosing human-owned company/project may have an external S5; no positive credit is borrowed from it without a first-party return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/7dc521d53844dc1b72502475859823926e1fec20/assessments/tr/tram.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 7dc521d53844dc1b72502475859823926e1fec20\">7dc521d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T18:39:13+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bussard76/agentic-factory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Agentic Factory</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/bussard76/agentic-factory/tree/7059e8bf028584045ce1274672eca7d50d185e9f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7059e8bf028584045ce1274672eca7d50d185e9f\">7059e8b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Produce software/task outcomes through model-directed worker execution and repairs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code, failing requirements/tests, implementation ambiguity and incomplete task results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer chooses implementation/verification actions within the bounded spec; responds to real tool/test/reviewer feedback and returns a committed result or escalation. - Decision owner: First-party specified Developer worker model in its own task worktree. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Orca worker-start, model CLI, task spec, branch/worktree, commands/gates, event/reply transport and Controller. - Closure path: Ready spec → generated developer instructions → start worker → model action/test/commit loop → workerdone or escalation → Tester/rework → reviewed integration. - Boundary reachability: init renders/copies the handbooks/spec scripts and start launches the Controller; its loop calls mkspec/start for actual workers. No custom organization prompt must be invented beyond supported installation/configuration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides how to implement and repair the assigned outcome; scripts transport/enforce those choices. Using an external CLI does not import that CLI own metasystem. - Evidence: bin/agentic-factory.mjs; controller/CONTROLLER.md; controller/start.mjs; controller/templates/mkspec.mjs; docs/roles.md; docs/workflow.md - Basis: explicit and structural supported installed mode -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate conflicting independently built changes and shared test-capacity interference among workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Separate developers produce branches that conflict or fail when combined; concurrent gates can saturate the host and amplify waits/timeouts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Controller chooses which ready/rework/testing tasks may proceed, groups integration waves and routes merge/integration conflict back to the responsible Developer and fresh Tester. - Decision owner: First-party Controller model, with task Developer making bounded corrective code choices after cross-task feedback. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate worktrees, measured capacity verdict, fair gate slots, serialized integration worktree, wave failure signals and retained review branches. - Closure path: Independent worker reports/branches → Controller selects wave/order under contention → integration reveals conflict or combined failure → task Developer resolves on its branch → fresh Tester checks → Controller retries a coherent wave. Pressure/gate observations also change later worker launch selection. - Boundary reachability: Rendered coordinator policy explicitly sends merge conflicts to the task Developer/Tester; Controller loop and start/wave scripts implement the launched-worker/event/combined-state path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses the concrete coordination/rework response from cross-worker state; worktrees, slots and numerical verdicts only isolate/enforce it. Delegation or sequencing alone is not credited. - Distinct S1 units: Independently executing Developers and Testers on separate task branches/worktrees sharing an eventual main and finite gate host. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Incompatible simultaneous changes at integration and contention between heavy test workers. - Attenuating coordination relation: Controller-managed wave/rework convergence, fresh verification of conflict repairs and capacity-aware launch selection. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Affected Developer receives conflict/failure work and changes its branch; Tester rechecks that repair, and later workers/waves are delayed or selected based on current contention. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It closes named cross-worker code-combination and host-contention disturbances, not merely a parent task decomposition. - Evidence: templates/docs/coordinator.md; controller/CONTROLLER.md; controller/start.mjs; controller/wave.mjs; controller/integ-wave.sh; controller/capacity.mjs; controller/gate.mjs; docs/workflow.md - Basis: explicit and structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate current fleet commitments, priorities, resource use, rework and integration on behalf of the factory project. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Stalled workers, failed rounds, quota/pressure changes, near-finished work, critical-path blockage and conflicting current demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Autonomous Controller chooses current worker/rework/wave allocation and bounded level-1/2 interventions; owner may change whole-project goals/priorities/resource rules through the supervised mode. - Decision owner: Base: Controller model inside recorded rules. Parent: legitimate owner via Front/status/inbox; these are distinct bounded control decisions, not simultaneous ownership of one verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workers.json, event wait/stallwatch, capacity/quotas, start/wave/cleanup, status table, inbox and decision records. - Closure path: Base reads worker events, capacity and queue, selects priority/rework/escalation/start, observes the next outcomes and revises. Parent sees full project status/needs, returns a priority or resource/commitment decision through Front/inbox, and Controller processes it into subsequent worker operations and records it. - Boundary reachability: init/start installs and starts the specified Controller with all support scripts; Front is installed to the owner host and explicitly relays answers/orders. Both loops are first-party supported handbooks, not arbitrary user callbacks. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The Controller owns discretionary task selection and intervention; fixed meters, caps and scripts do not own those choices. Parent reserved/current directives are reconstructed separately rather than inferred from ordinary Tester approval. - Whole-system current view: Controller worker table, queue/handover, completed/passed/blocked tasks, current gate/capacity/quota readings, status and owner inbox. - Current-control decision scope: Critical-path prioritization, allocation among rework/Testers/waves/new work, bounded task decisions, stalls/cleanup and owner-returned current priorities/resource rules. - Evidence: controller/CONTROLLER.md; front-agent/FRONTAGENT.md; docs/roles.md; controller/start.mjs; controller/wait.mjs; controller/stallwatch.mjs; controller/capacity.mjs; templates/docs/coordinator.md - Basis: explicit and structural multi-mode closure - Confidence: high - Caveats: Deterministic capacity calibration is enforcement/current regulation, not autonomous model ownership by itself. Owner-reserved level-3/policy matters remain outside Controller discretion.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complementarily challenge the factory control/reporting picture through independent pipeline evidence and session inspection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Routine worker/status reports can hide failure clusters, ineffective process changes, wasted capacity and growing risk. - Decisive decision or feedback right: A separately instantiated read-only Auditor/Retro evaluates raw journal/load/session evidence, judges causes/risks and prior-improvement metrics, and returns corrective findings. - Decision owner: Model-backed Pipeline Auditor/Retro for the audit judgment; Controller/owner decides the permitted corrective response. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Nightly/cluster triggers, audit/retro spec generator, separate worker session, journal/load/session sources, audit artifacts and next-audit metric comparison. - Closure path: Nightly or failure-cluster trigger → separate auditor reads journal/load/reviews/previous audits, or Retro reads raw worker sessions → measured findings/proposals enter status/handover → Controller runs permitted improvement tasks or escalates to owner → later audit checks the metric and ineffective changes are reverted. - Boundary reachability: Controller handbook mandates audit/retro launches; mkspec emits the role-specific evidence/questions/output contract and start launches the actual worker. init renders these scripts with real paths. This does not rely on developer-only factory-repository reviews. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Parsing/logging alone does not judge the claim. The separate model makes evidence-grounded audit judgments, and findings have a prescribed corrective return. Routine per-task Tester QA is not used as the sole S3 witness. - Claim being audited: Factory process/status explanations and claims that recent improvements improved operation, rather than merely whether a task test passes. - Ordinary reporting path: workerdone, task reports, Controller status and routine review verdicts. - Complementary access path: Independent recomputation over event journal, host load log, reports/reviews/debt and raw worker session moments, compared with prior audit success metrics. - Independence boundary: Separate read-only audit/retro worker cannot fix the code it judges; it has evidence beyond ordinary summarized status. Controller selects triggers and receives findings, so this is complementary operational audit, not institutional independence from all management. - Who acts on findings: Controller owns bounded level-1 corrective tasks and rollback; owner decides changes to costs/capacity/process policy. - Evidence: docs/improvement.md; controller/CONTROLLER.md; controller/templates/mkspec.mjs; controller/start.mjs; controller/journal.mjs; controller/loadlog.sh; docs/roles.md - Basis: explicit and structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Read-only is a role/protocol separation and runtime-dependent control, not a proven universal security boundary. No real audit or causal improvement result was executed here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No material first-party outside-and-then intelligence path at the declared supported boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Internal throughput/failure learning, fixed-tier model calibration and user-directed feature design can change current work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying function-level decision/feedback right reconstructed after checking supported positive and eligible parent modes. - Decision owner: No qualifying owner at this function; narrower operational or configuration choices remain as described. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Nightly internal audit, retros, fast-lane repairs, measured tier/host calibration, outside-in feature design and generic architecture/research skills. - Closure path: No function-specific returned closure is supplied by the checked paths. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Audit/retro/calibration use current internal failures, load and preconfigured model tiers; they do not establish externally/prospectively grounded factory capability options and an S3 conversation. Outside-in requirements/architecture decisions produce the software the factory is organized to build (S1 at this recursion), not a supplied strategic adaptation loop for the factory organization. Parent process decisions and generic research tools do not add a distinct S4 mode without that witness. - Evidence: docs/improvement.md; controller/calibrate-models.mjs; controller/capacity.mjs; front-agent/FRONTAGENT.md; docs/outside-in.md; templates/docs/coordinator.md - Basis: structural bounded absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Maintain the factory purpose, hard risk/data boundaries and ultimate rule/authority envelope under its legitimate owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Proposed changes to governing instructions, protected guardrails, role authority or permitted real-world effects exceed bounded worker/Controller rights. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Owner accepts/rejects a governing-policy/authority proposal and authorizes the returned durable rule version. - Decision owner: Project owner; Front asks/relays and Controller records/applies within that mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Needs-owner questions, status/inbox return, options/recommendation ADR, accepted/Decided-by provenance, protected-file commit checks, authoritative project docs and subsequent worker spec generation. - Closure path: Policy/authority matter is reserved and raised with options → Front obtains legitimate owner choice → answer returns through inbox → accepted owner-attributed ADR and governing docs record the choice → protected change references it → Controller/worker handbooks and later specs operate under the changed rule. - Boundary reachability: The rendered handbooks explicitly reserve human rules and authority; first-party Front/Controller return and decision-record paths ship with protected-file checks. This is more than a static AGENTS file or a task permission prompt. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Controller may repair internal templates but cannot decide the owner risk/policy envelope. The parent owns the verdict; a local hook checks documentation shape and supports its effect rather than supplying legitimacy itself. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Factory no-outward-effects/real-data boundary, protected governing instructions and who may alter its hard rules or authority; ordinary level-3 design tasks are not automatically treated as S5. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Legitimate human project owner for governing-policy decisions; no autonomous ultimate-policy mode established. - Return-to-operation path: Owner decision is relayed, recorded in accepted ADR/coordinator/workflow documents and applied to following Controller/worker operations; protected-file hooks require that recorded decision for governing changes. - Evidence: templates/AGENTS.md; controller/CONTROLLER.md; front-agent/FRONTAGENT.md; docs/decisions.md; templates/tools/factory-guardrails.mjs; templates/docs/adr/0001-adopt-agentic-factory.md; bin/agentic-factory.mjs - Basis: explicit and structural parent-governed policy closure - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Local hooks are cooperative controls, and a Decided-by line is not cryptographic proof of legitimate consent. The assessment credits the explicit supported owner-governed protocol, not tamper-proof enforcement or every architecture approval as S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/dca56ca83a560f32ec69e102f3fd6fe4b7c0692d/assessments/ag/agentic-factory.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit dca56ca83a560f32ec69e102f3fd6fe4b7c0692d\">dca56ca &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:09:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MarioMagdy/foreman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">foreman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/MarioMagdy/foreman/tree/219bf0961fa4c67caaa4e371fae63e497092d9b2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 219bf0961fa4c67caaa4e371fae63e497092d9b2\">219bf09 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute concrete plan stages through separate delegated coding agents; inspect files, edit code, run tests and return evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable implementation and test outcomes, technical obstacles, task requirements and repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and implement code changes and local fixes within each stage brief; interpret tool/test feedback. - Decision owner: The delegated model-bearing builder session, constrained by Foreman&#39;s brief, approved plan, host tools and owner safety policy. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git worktrees, stage branches, host CLI, brief/progress/mailbox/handoff files, deterministic runner and stage gate. - Closure path: Approved stage brief → builder model inspects repository → code edits/tests → returns handback and changes → foreman independently checks and issues accept or fix feedback. - Boundary reachability: Both installed host adapters explicitly launch agent CLI/subagent workers in prepared worktrees; this is a normal Foreman mode, not a README-only imaginary integration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Workers choose substantive code and tool behavior. Neither the launcher nor the human-written stage brief predetermines implementation decisions. - Evidence: SKILL stage loop, generic host launch instructions, delegation setup. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the supported mode; no live provider-backed run was executed here. - Caveats: A bare installation of files into an otherwise idle CLI does nothing autonomously; the host model and owner-approved run must be active.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Parallel builder sessions in separate stage worktrees and, in track mode, separate manager-supervised build streams. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two tracks editing shared contracts or dependency interfaces; premature consumption of ungated upstream stage output; quota failure cascading across shared worker/reviewer accounts; conflicting worktree integration. - Attenuating coordination relation: Foreman designs dependency/critical-path tracks and limits effective parallelism; track managers see per-stage gate states, coordinate cross-track facts via shared append-only files and explicitly wait/rebase when provisional dependencies change. Contract changes and loosening gates must escalate to the orchestrator/owner instead of silently racing. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The manager inspects cross-track shared notes, dependency and gate status, adjusts when an interface changes, sends a revised builder brief or rebase/test requirement, and can hold a stage pending the responsible decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The path addresses observed inter-worker interference over shared contracts, concurrent write ownership and ungated dependency consumption, and returns corrective replanning to affected workers; the event store itself is support only. - Function: Attenuate collisions and timing conflicts among simultaneously acting stages and tracks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incompatible contracts, premature downstream commitments, concurrent file ownership and shared-model quota contention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Tighten the stage dependency/contract handling, defer or provisionally start work, require rebase and re-verification, reroute workers after a shared-resource failure. - Decision owner: Foreman/orchestrator model in the default mode and autonomous track-manager model under its approved charter in track mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: One-writer track files, append-only shared notes, gate IDs, milestone branches, worktrees and launcher capacity limits. - Closure path: Conflicting dependency/worker state → manager detects and records the shared issue → chooses reordering or corrective contract handling → affected worker brief/ready state changes → gate verifies the resolved output. - Boundary reachability: Track mode is a supported built-in mode selected for sufficiently wide runs, and charter, event contracts and host adapters are shipped and installed. It requires an active host model, not an unshipped external coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The manager model chooses how to respond to cross-track disturbances; worktree isolation and deterministic launcher limits do not themselves make the corrective decision. - Evidence: architect pass/concurrent track rules, manager coordination/contract escalation, harness adapter. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; the prescribed return path is clear, but a live collision-recovery replay was not performed. - Caveats: Single-stage runs need no inter-unit S2. Strict contract changes remain human/orchestrator-authorized, not discretionary manager edits.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Whole-system current view: Foreman maintains the approved plan, all milestone/stage dependencies, worktree/branch state, per-gate evidence, worker handbacks, open questions and the tracker. The human can view the same run and answer its staffing and acceptance questions. - Current-control decision scope: Select critical path and parallel tracks, assign workers and reviewers, schedule and retry stages, choose corrective briefs and alternate models; the parent separately approves resource/role arrangements and controls milestone integration. - Function: Govern active stage and team commitments so independent outputs form a single usable milestone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing dependencies, failed tests, worker hang/quota, resource pressure, reviewer objections, owner priorities and integration seams. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Autonomous mode allocates stage assignments and fix rounds under standing orders; parent mode accepts or changes the run sheet&#39;s model staffing, resource caps, branch authority and release of the current milestone. - Decision owner: Foreman/orchestrator or track-manager model for authorized current work; human owner for the approved run&#39;s global staffing/constraints and milestone acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run sheet, plan.yaml, briefs, staffing.yaml, gates, worktrees, tracker, events and launcher signals. - Closure path: Current run/worker evidence → foreman selects commitments or fallback → worker/branch state changes and reports back; parent reviews proposed staffing/plan in run sheet or a milestone packet → approval/changes enter standing orders and govern subsequent workers/merges. - Boundary reachability: Both Foreman&#39;s autonomous stage loop and owner-approved run-sheet/milestone gate are mandatory operating paths in the installed skill; the parent return path has explicit decision/answer events, not a mere UI button. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-controlled work allocation establishes the autonomous base; the human&#39;s explicit approval of staffing and ongoing milestone integration supplies a distinct legitimate parent control mode. - Evidence: architect pass/run sheet, manager current-control charter, owner gate return. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high, with no claim of measured staffing quality. - Caveats: The parent mode is not inferred from a generic PR merge button; it includes pre-run resource allocation and explicit run-level choices consumed by the whole manager and workers.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Builder&#39;s assertion that a stage/milestone satisfies test criteria and that its PR is ready to merge. - Ordinary reporting path: Builder writes a DONE/UNSURE/BLOCKED handback and commits source changes to its stage branch. - Complementary access path: Non-builder Foreman/track manager inspects Git status/diff, runs actual tests and probes the app independently; a separate reviewer model reads the change and criteria at stage and milestone gates, recording findings before fixes. - Independence boundary: The verifier/reviewer is explicitly forbidden to be the original builder for that stage; raw repository/test evidence is available rather than solely the worker&#39;s narrative. In track mode the manager never builds; reviewer routing is separated by role. - Who acts on findings: Foreman/manager rules on each finding, writes a new fix brief, dispatches it to the implementation worker, and re-verifies the changed result; owner receives the integrated packet. - Function: Challenge and correct produced work using complementary evidence under a non-builder decision right. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False builder-success claims, hidden integration failures, unnoticed branch changes and ineffective fixes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independent verifier/reviewer judges actual changes, issues an accept/fix verdict and sends corrective findings. - Decision owner: Model-bearing Foreman or track manager and separately dispatched non-builder reviewer; deterministic tests provide data, not the autonomous finding decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git diffs, stage tests, review gates, fixed test hashes, PR comments, independent verifier briefs and re-review. - Closure path: Builder handback → independent raw-code/test inspection → reviewer findings and foreman rulings → new fix assignment → raw-diff re-check → gate passes or escalates. - Boundary reachability: SKILL stage loop and first-party gate reference require the non-builder and actual source/test paths in the installed, user-approved Foreman operation; optional third-party reviewers are not needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An agent other than the producer interprets complementary evidence and chooses corrective findings; neither CI green nor self-reported builder status is substituted for that judgment. - Evidence: independent verification/gate loop, gate criteria/reviewer, manager not-builder rule. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for prescribed independent evidence access and corrective return, without empirical reliability claims. - Caveats: A separately installed council-judge skill is optional; this claim rests on core Foreman non-builder review, not on that optional dependency.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No installed prospective scanning/learning-and-adaptation function separate from executing and correcting this run is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing future product/environment demands are discussed in planning, but not autonomously converted into durable future organization capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choosing future changes to operating repertoire after observing outside trends; no such first-party durable decision owner found. - Decision owner: Foreman performs task-specific planning and post-gate fix loops; human can change local defaults or create a new run, which does not by itself establish S4 return. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run sheets, LOCAL.md, optional explore spikes, study scripts, reports and retrospective observations. - Closure path: Fix rounds adapt present work; new-run policy/workflow adoption from prospective intelligence is not an installed closed loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-mediated adaptation inside current stages is S3; comparison-eval reports and owner-made future ideas are adjacent development surfaces. - Evidence: explore and run lifecycle, study evaluator, local overlay example. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium-high for the declared installed-run boundary. - Caveats: External owner may deliberately learn from final reports across runs, but no first-party binding feedback/update of future capability is established.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: The installed run has task and authority rules, but no separate first-party ultimate identity/policy decision-and-return loop beyond its current plan/control is demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in owner task/scope, permission, gate strictness and model staffing are handled in S3; no independent self-definition/governance cycle is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Alter the enterprise&#39;s ultimate purpose, membership constitution or binding identity-policy rather than scheduling this project; no distinct runtime S5 owner/return found. - Decision owner: The owner chooses the project and run settings; Foreman applies a fixed normative skill identity and approved standing orders without autonomous ultimate-policy revision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Run sheet, LOCAL.md house rules, immutable role charters, owner question/decision events and deployment gates. - Closure path: Task-specific authorization and policy enforcement enter S3 commitments; a separate identity/policy correction returned to changed future operation is not established at the stated run recursion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-facing skill rules prohibit self-upgrading merge/prod powers, and humans changing ordinary project constraints are not automatically ownership of organization-level S5. - Evidence: fixed Foreman identity and standing rules, local overlay, manager authority limits. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; bounded to the installed project-run organization. - Caveats: S5 would require stronger source evidence of an authoritative identity/purpose decision, not merely a user-gated code merge or configurable agent role.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/0ac25984abc6b99cc39de62917948ceb291a4e93/assessments/ma/mariomagdy-foreman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 0ac25984abc6b99cc39de62917948ceb291a4e93\">0ac2598 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T20:31:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rudderlabs/devbox\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DevBox</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rudderlabs/devbox/tree/fc2b4270eb803404281e352bb79394a54d28c723\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fc2b4270eb803404281e352bb79394a54d28c723\">fc2b427 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: implement assigned engineering changes in one repository through a model-backed agent using source/test feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code paths, failed checks, requirements ambiguity, repo-local implementation issues and API/CI tool responses. - Decisive decision or feedback right: per-repo DeepAgents implementation model selects edits, commands, corrections and structured completion evidence. - Decision owner: configured implementation model; planning models similarly own local repository plan work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DeepAgentSession, repo/tool middleware, stage model routing, persisted thread/checkpointer, sandbox workspace and structured result schema. - Closure path: assigned repo plan → model code/tool actions → observed results → recorded implementation.json → independent review/fix or PR publication. - Boundary reachability: normal bun run up + control-plane assignment inbox enters handlers.assignment and constructs per-repo model stage agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sandbox/inbox scheduler has no model/tool discretion; the implementation agent decides the actual code change and follows test feedback. - Evidence: implementation, implementation agent, assignment. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for installed task-to-model action path; provider task quality not empirically measured. - Caveats: requires configured LLM keys and external target repos; CI/test results can still fail.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate concrete cross-repository interface/contract mismatch between distinct concurrently authored repo plans before implementation cells act on incompatible assumptions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independently planned repo components can omit or contradict dependencies at cross-repo boundaries or miss a required repository. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate plan-review model examines the assembled multi-repo plan for missing cross-repo contracts/interfaces and generates repo-scoped issues; the harness returns those issues to their planning agents or instantiates a missing repo planner for a corrected plan. - Decision owner: plan-review model judges inter-repo incompatibility; deterministic planning driver performs routing to the affected model S1 planners. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parallel per-repository PlanningAgent sessions, shared crossrepoassumptions, PlanReviewAgent output, issuesbyrepo and per-repo revision prompts. - Closure path: concurrent repo plan outputs → merged plan and cross-repo assumptions → reviewer detects interface gap → issue returned to affected repo planner/new repo → revised plan governs later implementation. - Boundary reachability: ordinary multi-repo assignment path runs orientation, per-repo parallel planning and plan review; no external plugin mediation needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer model makes substantive cross-repo compatibility judgment; the Python loop transports that feedback but cannot invent missing interfaces on its own. - Distinct S1 units: separate model PlanningAgent sessions for independently scoped repositories, followed by distinct implementation agents for each planned repository. - Inter-S1 disturbance: missing cross-repository contracts and interfaces or omitted collaborating repo in the combined plan, arising from independent repo-level planning assumptions. - Attenuating coordination relation: explicit whole-plan cross-repository reviewer issues are assigned back to the specific source planner, including a new planner for an omitted repo. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: reporevisionprompt reruns the affected planner with its issues, and the revised output replaces the earlier plan before implementation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this path targets incompatibility of the outputs of independent repo units, not just a task queue or passive shared state. - Evidence: planning.py, plan reviewer. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for shipped inter-repo feedback path; no live cross-repo incompatibility reproduction performed. - Caveats: review permits unresolved unscoped issues and bounds revision passes; model can miss interface conflicts. S2 claim is restricted to the cross-repo planning relation, not generic cross-assignment sandbox orchestration.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: choose and repair the current assignment&#39;s whole repository scope and operating commitments before dispatch to implementation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task intent, omitted repos, revised cross-repo obligations and missing validation/implementation scope. - Decisive decision or feedback right: orientation model selects which repositories/components are in the assignment; whole-plan review model can identify additional repository commitments or force current plan revision, which is returned to the stage dispatcher. - Decision owner: model orientation/whole-plan review roles, with deterministic stage runner enforcing and composing their selected assignments. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OrientationAgent, PlanningAgent fanout, PlanReviewAgent, structured PlanRepo/PlanReviewOutput, revision/new-repo dispatch and per-task state. - Closure path: whole workspace/task view → model scope selection → current multi-repo agents plan → whole-plan model judgment/reallocation → revised repo plan → actual implementation dispatch. - Boundary reachability: standard multi-repo assignment mode from the first-party inbox handler; no manual synthesis of a higher model required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the orientation/whole-plan reviewer models leaves deterministic scripts unable to decide which current repo commitments the assignment needs or when absent work must be added. - Whole-system current view: orientation sees task and workspace repository inventory; plan reviewer receives whole compiled plan and cross-repository assumptions, not one isolated S1&#39;s local output. - Current-control decision scope: selecting involved repos/components, commissioning added repo planning, revising current task implementation/validation commitments before execution. - Evidence: planning.py, orientation agent, assignment.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high, specifically for the bounded current assignment rather than all organization tasks. - Caveats: the wider control plane&#39;s concurrent sandbox scheduling and budgets are deterministic, not a model-run global S3; the S3 claim is confined to one multi-repository assignment, not whole-company management.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge plan and code/change claims, generate findings and return them into corrective execution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: planning omissions, code bugs, failed contracts, implementation bias, ungrounded severity and missed review issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate PlanReviewAgent adjudicates the draft plan; post-implementation ReviewLensAgent models inspect actual working-tree diffs, emit grounded findings and return those to new ReviewFixAgent sessions. - Decision owner: model reviewers in distinct read-only sessions/threads, never the implementation author; deterministic harness merges and caps reports but does not choose substantive code findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: non-durable adversarial plan reviewer, per-(repo,lens) review thread, code-review/bug-hunter/security lenses, merge-findings agent, runselfreviewloop, per-repo fix sessions and revision/fingerprint checks. - Closure path: draft plan/diff → independent model judgments → plan-agent revisions or review-fix code actions → reviewed state loop or surfaced unresolved PR warnings. - Boundary reachability: plan review in standard assignment and self-review enabled by default; no dogfood-only actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer models independently read source/diffs and choose findings; the executor/controller only routes them. Distinct implementation and reviewer threads protect against self-certification. - Claim being audited: planning sufficiency and correctness/security of the implementation diff before draft PR publication. - Ordinary reporting path: planning agents and implementation agents produce plan/implementation artifacts and pass claims. - Complementary access path: read-only PlanReviewAgent sees workspace and whole plan; separate lens agents read git diff including untracked changes and independently trace code paths. - Independence boundary: one reviewer thread per repository/lens never inherits implementer or other reviewer thread; fix agent also uses a fresh thread rather than the author&#39;s. - Who acts on findings: plan driver re-dispatches planners; self-review fix agent triages/fixes or records explicit declines, and the harness iterates or annotates unresolved warnings. - Evidence: plan reviewer, review lens agent, self-review. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for independent model sessions and corrective return; live review quality not tested. - Caveats: review stage is opt-out, missing isolated lens result can degrade to empty unless all fail, and declined/iteration-exhausted findings may be published with warnings rather than stopping all PRs.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate externally/future-grounded organizational option-development loop proven beyond task memory distillation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: historical project mistakes/feedback inform future code tasks, but no explicit future environmental uncertainty or alternatives are sensed and evaluated for system capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for outside-and-then strategic adaptation. - Decision owner: none established for S4; task distillation model selects high-signal existing notes, not prospective strategy options. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: staged harness memory, DistillationAgent, versioned repository .agents/knowledge files and future-session knowledge middleware. - Closure path: past-task note → knowledge promotion → later agent context; this learning path lacks an externally grounded prospective choice/return warranting S4. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model filtering and persisting repository conventions from current implementation evidence is durable memory maintenance, not a demonstrated foresight/adaptation organization. - Evidence: distillation, distillation agent, architecture. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: future task productivity might improve, but this is not evidence that S4 future options are developed and returned to S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no distinct ultimate identity/organizational policy decision-and-return loop established inside the harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repo authorization, credential access, human PR approval and scope changes are execution/task constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none demonstrated for ultimate identity policy. - Decision owner: none established for S5; operators select repos, keys and final merges outside the AI harness decision graph. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: repo allowlist and iron-proxy permissions, configuration, per-task sandbox isolation, human PR merge and review API. - Closure path: no identity-level proposal → legitimate institutional decision → updated ultimate-policy return into ongoing harness. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 mode claimed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: merge permission, token allowlist and per-PR comments are ordinary operating governance, not a first-party identity-policy body. - Evidence: architecture, README, control plane. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: real organizations may operate policy/identity above deployed DevBox, which cannot be inferred from this repository&#39;s tasks.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/40b8f4a4a23a1571cac550df27206ed42c9ece77/assessments/de/devbox.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 40b8f4a4a23a1571cac550df27206ed42c9ece77\">40b8f4a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T16:56:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tauhb/aris-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">aris-agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/tauhb/aris-agent/tree/067695907ffa776afdf42595b08a7587e0c00424\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 067695907ffa776afdf42595b08a7587e0c00424\">0676959 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform autonomous user-requested work through model-selected tools and independently acting bot sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different user requests, source/tool feedback, missing information, task progress and local failures. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose a substantive tool/response or delegated task action, interpret results and continue/stop within a goal. - Decision owner: The model-bearing current bot and assigned operational bot, using the installed DSH loop and Aris-defined team/task context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DSH model/tool/session engine, Aris prompt and bot roster, guard, goal counters, state stores and bounded scheduling. - Closure path: User/goal → bot model turn → tool or teammate action → observed result → next turn/action and final response or goal disposition. - Boundary reachability: Standard Aris composition includes the live model/tool DSH engine, with Aris goal/team integrations across web/headless/ACP modes; delegation is an installed callable tool rather than an unshipped plan. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model chooses task content and actions; the runtime may enforce permissions or persistence but does not make the operational choice. - Evidence: bundle wiring, Commander tool and prompt, dispatch/teammate action, goal continuation and verdict. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the wired agent/action path; runtime provider execution was not replayed in this review. - Caveats: Aris does not own the external model or DSH&#39;s independent general-purpose agent engine, but its installed product organizes their participation in this task boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Named concurrently active team bots, each with an independent task/session and potentially overlapping skills or dependencies. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Duplicate assignments to the same bot, overcommitting the shared busy-bot capacity, dispatching not-ready dependency cards, and two workers attempting incompatible task ownership. - Attenuating coordination relation: Commander selects a target and action from the roster and observed task state; dispatch refuses duplicate names, rejects capacity overflow and dependency-unready cards, records owner/revision and returns explicit refusal feedback. The model must sequence or revise assignments in response. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Rejected or blocked dispatch is reported back in the model tool result; Commander is instructed to wait for dependencies or worker results, then choose another assignment, sequence later work and integrate reports. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Specific shared-worker overcommitment and dependency collisions are tested before dispatch; refusal is an in-band signal for changed subsequent team commitments, not merely arbitrary message transport. - Function: Attenuate team-agent assignment/interference and dependency oscillation while keeping work distributed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competition for a limited set of specialist workers and premature dependent-task starts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select an alternative worker, revise assignment ordering, or postpone dependent work upon real conflict feedback. - Decision owner: Model-driven Commander/authorized lead for task reallocation; deterministic checks enforce task-ready, single-target and max-busy constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DSH task/member state, busyBots, repeated-handle check, task revision and owner update, and the explicit rejection summary. - Closure path: Competing assignment → guard refuses or task reports not-ready → model receives error → revises dispatch/waits → subsequent worker commitments reflect the new choice. - Boundary reachability: Both Commander model guidance and concrete dispatch tool/board checks ship in the configured Aris bundle, with no separately scripted user glue needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Hard capacity/duplicate checks do not themselves choose the new work partition; that reallocation remains the model&#39;s discretion. - Evidence: dispatch interference guards and return, Commander sequencing guidance/tool registration, project board. - Basis: structural + explicit. - Confidence: medium; first-party paths are present, but no observed multi-bot collision-recovery success rate is claimed. - Caveats: If only one bot operates, this inter-bot function is dormant. Mechanical rejection is supporting S2 rather than independent agentic ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Commander has the team roster, work/assignment history, busy count, project tasks and returned worker status; an authorized lead can view its department&#39;s present work. - Current-control decision scope: Decide current task granularity, which specialist/department receives work, staged commitments, shared capacity use, dependency sequencing and integration of worker results. - Function: Govern current shared team commitments and accountable delegation against the user&#39;s whole task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Insufficient specialist fit, busy worker capacity, dependencies, incomplete or failed delegated tasks, changing immediate user priorities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: From the full requested task and team state, choose direct work vs delegation vs department, specialist/commitment allocation and response to returned results. - Decision owner: Model-bearing Commander or the delegated department-lead session for the relevant current work; human explicitly chooses or overrides its own requests. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamPort and DSH member/task APIs, recorded dispatch, limits and task Driver, budgets, authorization settings. - Closure path: User goal and live team context → Commander selects whole-task resource commitments → task/member state and bot actions update → returned reports/errors prompt next current allocation or completed answer. - Boundary reachability: The standard Commander plugin injects its current-control guide and registers giaoviec/department delegation; dependency and status hooks are wired into the operational team path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent selects which current work and roles to commit, while the task board, concurrency limits and guard merely implement/limit that discretion. - Evidence: Commander guidance and live ports, assignment choices and records, projects. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for available current-control authority; the quality of resource decisions was not empirically benchmarked. - Caveats: A per-task assignment alone would be insufficient; the whole-team/project state and controlled commitments supply the narrower S3 claim. Operator preferences and hard caps are not transferred to the agent.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No separate sufficiently independent complementary inspection and corrective audit channel is established in the reviewed installed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential false task/goal completion and misleading agent reports, mitigated partly by ordinary goal grading, tests and checkpoints but without an independent audit owner. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently challenge an operational claim using alternative reality evidence and send a corrective finding; no such first-party governed right found. - Decision owner: The after-turn aris-autonomy judge decides goal status from the bot&#39;s own answer/tool-name record; deterministic checkpoints/guard do not exercise independent operational audit judgment. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model-based completion grader, file snapshots, ordinary skill evaluations and logs. - Closure path: Goal verdict can block/retry the same task, but its inputs are mainly the producer&#39;s ordinary report; a complementary ground-truth-to-control return is not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A distinct model call for a routine completion verdict is not inherently independent access to reality; checkpoints capture recovery material, not an audit finding owner. - Evidence: judge inputs and goal transitions, pre-tool file checkpoints, skill evaluation surface. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the pinned installed modes. - Caveats: An operator may configure external review, but no first-party routine auditor with complementary source access and returned corrective scope was demonstrated.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - External distinction: Actual user corrections, remember-this requests, repeated failures, tool outcomes and observed individual preferences enter the reflection engine from task encounters; these are environmental feedback beyond mere current-step planning. - Future / prospective distinction: Retained user insights alter future bot contexts; proposed reusable memories/skills are intended for subsequent tasks, not just finishing the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: A model review extracts structured preferences and reusable task practice; it may propose a new or updated skill with evaluation examples, while high-confidence owner insights are recorded automatically. - Path back into current capability / S3: Trusted observations are rendered into later system-prompt context; a human-approved skill becomes active in the catalog and available to later agents. - Function: Convert observed environmental/use feedback into a changed future operating repertoire for the team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Recurring mismatch between user practice and existing agent guidance, newly revealed preferences and tool-failure patterns. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In the base mode the reflection model selects trusted future-relevant observations that Aris persists/loads; in the parent mode the user approves/rejects skill adaptation before activation. - Decision owner: Reflection model in the autonomous owner-insight path; owner/user in the reviewed-skill publication path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Trigger heuristics, provider selection, daily cost cap, insight confidence store, proposal queue, skill catalog and prompt-context renderer. - Closure path: User corrections/tool history → reflect model makes future guidance → high-confidence insight automatically enters subsequent prompt; alternatively skill proposal → owner approval → active skills refresh → subsequent task may use the new skill. - Boundary reachability: Reflection and skills plugins are in the standard bundle, subscribed to actual sessions and wired to context and review/approval API, not test-only self-improvement examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: In base insight mode the model makes the substantive content selection and persistence is automatic; in skill mode the user holds acceptance discretion. Neither the queue nor the scheduler owns that choice. - Evidence: reflection triggers and insight feedback, insight confidence/prompt, skill proposal, approval and live refresh. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; the mechanism is installed but not behaviorally measured across real future tasks. - Caveats: These are user- and experience-oriented adaptation modes; routine in-goal progress verdicts alone are not credited as S4. Unapproved skill drafts cannot be credited as active capability.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: What a named team bot is for, its assigned role/persona and binding department tool policy; an Aris rewrite can propose an updated identity, but does not self-authorize it. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The self-hosted installation owner, through authenticated bot/department API and human confirmation, rather than the generated persona text or model that drafts it. - Return-to-operation path: User updates or accepts rewritten SOUL/role/department policy → bot store and version history change → required prompt-context renderer and tool policy read the new binding settings for later sessions/actions. - Function: Close the purpose, identity and binding rule choices for the owned bot team. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting or changing user expectations about agent role, working ethos, and allowed tools relative to actual tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Accept, replace or restore the bot&#39;s identity instructions and binding departmental tool permissions. - Decision owner: Parent installation owner/user; the optional model-driven rewrite returns only an uncommitted draft. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: soul-rewrite draft model, versioned SOUL history, bot/departments store, authenticated API, required persona context, tool permission gate. - Closure path: Parent identifies desired role/policy update → model may suggest SOUL → user saves it via API → durable bot/department state changes → subsequent bot prompt and tool checks enforce the new identity/constraints. - Boundary reachability: Bot/departments API, soul-rewrite, history/version restore and required persona context are installed core product surfaces, not repository governance or static config examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the human decision leaves a draft but not an authoritative identity update; prompt injection and tool enforcement merely apply accepted parent choices. - Evidence: bot API identity edits and rewrite draft, SOUL revision history, required persona injection, policy enforcement. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium for the per-installation bot-team parent path, not for any broader community/open-source parent governance. - Caveats: Ordinary approval of a file edit, API credential or spending cap is not itself S5; this mapping is limited to role/SOUL/department-purpose decisions that define who each bot is. No independent autonomous agent-owned ultimate-policy mode is credited.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9e85b75ec9898a51fea52cf1f050f6e336d9d7f8/assessments/ar/aris-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9e85b75ec9898a51fea52cf1f050f6e336d9d7f8\">9e85b75 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T17:59:09+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FAHTHIZ/oasis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OASIS</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FAHTHIZ/oasis/tree/b03a4207c6969a71d570cedf4db2651245b4a822\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b03a4207c6969a71d570cedf4db2651245b4a822\">b03a420 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Local autonomous coding task execution with tool observations and completion feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Codebase uncertainty, tool errors, unexpected test failures, model tool-output malformation and incomplete edits. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Model selects and revises file/search/shell/Git/tool operations from task observations; may request child agents. - Decision owner: Chosen parent or task-specific model agent, not the provider endpoint or deterministic safety layer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runAgentTurnRaw step loop, executeTool dispatcher, permissions, tool-call repair, session history, file checkpoints and verification state. - Closure path: Model tool-call→runtime authorization/execution→tool result→subsequent model turn, with final response checked against actual diff/test evidence. - Boundary reachability: Normal shipped OASIS CLI/web/SDK session and task-subagent APIs, not an evaluation-only mock. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Host safety gates constrain and verify, but cannot choose the task-sensitive next action without the model. - Evidence: agent.mjs#L4633, agent.mjs#L5019, tools.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high for pinned first-party loop wiring, not independent quality/effectiveness. - Caveats: No paid/local model run was performed; model availability and permissions vary by deployment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Multiple model workers launched through task/background dispatch, with separate context and optional isolated worktrees. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two workers attempting to claim/change the same shared task, duplicate work after an expired claim, and downstream work starting before a prerequisite is complete. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared task leases/dependency checks plus a model-accessible tasklist, taskclaim, taskrelease, taskupdate, teamsendmessage and conditional dispatcher. Parent/model and workers can select alternative ready work or explicitly release/reassign after observing a conflict. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Claim rejection names current claimant/expiry, successful release/expiry opens a task for reassignment, and prerequisite status changes readiness for subsequent dispatch; agent receives these tool results before its next choice. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: Exclusive ownership and dependency constraints are designed to attenuate concrete conflicting work, not just order generic messages; conflict feedback alters which worker can perform which task next. - Function: Inter-worker mutual-exclusion, dependency and conflict-adjustment regulation at task-board recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Duplicate edits/claims, incompatible simultaneous work and premature dependent launches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select and adjust shared-task ownership/dependencies and reassign after claim refusal or worker failure. - Decision owner: Model agents exercising first-party board and team tools, including parent with explicit force-release authority; runtime owns only atomic lease enforcement and safe dispatch limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session-local board with lease TTL, dependency DAG, agent registry/status/events, per-worker worktrees and dispatch waves. - Closure path: Collision/expired claim or failed worker→board tool status and event to agent→new claim, release, reassignment or changed dependency→next eligible worker execution. - Boundary reachability: Shipped default task and dispatchreadytasks tools are exposed to live parent/worker sessions, not developer-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The code enforces accepted decisions, while autonomous agents choose task selection, claim/release and response to returned contention information; simply auto-serializing alone is not credited. - Evidence: shared-tasks.mjs#L232, agent.mjs#L3552, agent-tasks.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for the supported multi-agent mode; no reproduced competing-work test in this review. - Caveats: A session that never launches peers has no active S2 relation; leases are session-local and reset on process restoration. A failed automatic dispatch pauses; agent discretion to adjust is available but not guaranteed to be effective.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: Parent model can inspect the full shared task board, claims, dependencies and all worker reports/status/events within its session, including worktree changes awaiting integration. - Current-control decision scope: Create/prioritize task commitments, change dependencies/owners, choose dispatch parallelism, pause or resume waves, intervene in failures and judge whether to integrate worker branches. - Function: Current whole-session commitments and resource allocation across delegated code work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked dependencies, worker failure, depleted agent slots, pending unintegrated changes and unfinished work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The main model changes current task definitions/owners, manages waves and adjusts continuation from observed worker evidence. - Decision owner: Main OASIS model agent, subject to operator approval for unsafe tool/merge operations; runtime only enforces preselected guardrails. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tasklist/update, agentstatus/events, dispatch limit and pause logic, Git worktrees and explicit review/integration workflow. - Closure path: Workers finish/fail→agent event/board read→main model updates commitments or dispatches/reviews next wave→new worker state or a blocked escalation. - Boundary reachability: First-party task, agent and dispatch tools are available in an ordinary multi-agent OASIS session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The dispatcher computes feasible slots but does not decide task priorities, exception response or integration; those are model/current-control choices. - Evidence: agent.mjs#L549, agent.mjs#L3431, agent.mjs#L3552. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium; supported current-control topology, not measured resilience of a real swarm. - Caveats: No sustained service across process restart; external manual worktree integration is required. Human intervention rights at tool gates do not alone qualify a separate S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: Parent or implementation worker&#39;s claim that a code change is correct or free of relevant security regressions. - Ordinary reporting path: Implementing model returns its own final prose, tool transcript, diff and verification state to parent/main session. - Complementary access path: Distinct read-only code-review / security-review model session or bounded runspecialistreview directly reads actual repository files and Git evidence with independent tool context, then returns finding details. - Independence boundary: Specialist has a separate session, no inherited working conversation as authority, read-only tool allowlist and explicit prohibition on edits, shell/network and MCP; distinct built-in read-only reviewers can be invoked through task. This is procedural separation, not institutional isolation or a mandatory third-party audit. - Who acts on findings: Parent/main agent receives reviewer result in the normal tool response, inspects reported paths and applies or delegates corrections, followed by available tests/verification. - Function: Independent challenge of work claims from actual source/diff evidence, with returned corrective action potential. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Plausible but incorrect implementation summaries, untested edge cases and security regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separate specialist model judges inspected code and reports bounded actionable findings; main session owns remediation decisions. - Decision owner: Read-only review model owns independent audit judgment, distinct from production agent; parent owns current-control correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runSpecialistReview, specialist roles, bounded review budgets, reviewer read-only tool filter and ordinary verification gate. - Closure path: Main invokes review→separate model reads actual files/diff→returns concrete findings→main re-evaluates and revises code or task→checks again. - Boundary reachability: Built-in specialist-review tool and code-review/security-review subagents available without custom extensions, in normal coding sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The specialist—not the implementer or its deterministic verifier—chooses what to challenge and whether evidence supports a defect. - Evidence: agent.mjs#L2982, agent.mjs#L4211, subagents.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium for reachable complementary path; not a measured audit-success rate. - Caveats: Review is optional, and some tool/approval modes may narrow its usefulness. Deterministic auto-test and LSP checks are excluded from S3 credit by themselves.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Prospective external/environmental sensing and governing self-adaptation of the OASIS session capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving APIs, model catalogs and ecosystem plugins. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No evidenced first-party external future assessment→adaptation option→committed capability update loop under an autonomous S4 actor. - Decision owner: Operator/developer selects model, extension updates and imported skills; task-agent searches are for the current user deliverable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model discovery/router, web search, update command, plugin catalog, imported skill review, session memory and workflow scripts. - Closure path: Configuration/skills change by operator action or a current task&#39;s explicit command; there is no distinct prospective environmental function returning to whole-session S3 capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Availability of web tools, plugin import and a roadmap does not establish self-directed future adaptation of the harness. - Evidence: architecture, update.mjs, extensions.mjs. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high for bounded standard modes examined. - Caveats: A custom workflow may research future changes, but that composition is not a shipped autonomous S4 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Ultimate identity/policy conflict resolution for the OASIS agent system itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing goals and exceptional decisions over core system identity, not ordinary tool safety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party identity-level legitimacy mandate and binding returned policy decision. - Decision owner: User/developer authors permissions, model/tool settings, static policy text and task instructions; model cannot redefine ultimate authority through the workflow. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Permission policy, plan/approval gates, trust boundaries, security patterns and protected paths. - Closure path: Approval or rejection returns to individual tool/task execution; no identity dispute reaches a legitimized ultimate authority and returns to govern subsequent system purpose. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Even stringent deterministic safety checks regulate risky acts, not the identity or ultimate policy of a viable system. - Evidence: permissions.mjs, agent.mjs, architecture. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Human project governance outside the runtime is not asserted absent.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4224e16c244d760835866078fe52634c8283401f/assessments/oa/oasis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4224e16c244d760835866078fe52634c8283401f\">4224e16 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-03T20:24:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Jero110/pi-house\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pi-house</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-03\">2026-11-03</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Jero110/pi-house/tree/5c456584931025f421eb45c7232683737a628371\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5c456584931025f421eb45c7232683737a628371\">5c45658 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Carry out plan-scoped code tasks and evaluate local acceptance results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task-specific code state, unforeseen implementation obstacles, command results and integration errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The dispatched model worker selects edits, tools, tests and repairs within its stated task contract. - Decision owner: Individual model-bearing agent process working under the first-party executor/agent-link protocol. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Pinned plan, task brief, harness-native coding interface, isolated worktree, result routing and test commands. - Closure path: Task brief → worker inspects source → edits and tests → reports result/commit → executor verification and possible repair → accepted code. - Boundary reachability: The shipped planner/executor skills and agent-link spawn route instantiate this ordinary mode through installed pih, not through an unrelated downstream user application. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Workers adapt code/tool choices and failures; the hub and test executable alone do not choose fixes. Model cognition belongs to installed external runtimes but the task execution operating loop is explicitly first-party assembled. - Evidence: executor skill, Steps 1–3, architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for the shipped protocol; no provider-backed run executed for this review. - Caveats: External agent/model availability and the user&#39;s task quality remain outside ownership.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Distinct S1 units: Concurrent model-bearing coding workers, each assigned a task, branch/worktree, and owned files. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two otherwise valid workers editing shared files, violating prerequisite order, clashing during integration or exhausting a shared quota pool. - Attenuating coordination relation: The executor checks dependency and file independence before fanout, assigns disjoint ownership, defers shared wiring, revises dispatch after results and resolves isolated integration conflicts; the hub relays workers&#39; status and feedback. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: A conflicting or unverified result is not declared done; the executor revises task integration or asks the worker for a corrected result, and only independent ready tasks continue concurrently. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The executor skill names inter-worker file conflicts and dependent work as disturbances, requiring model-owned independence judgment and changed commitments, above deterministic message relay and FIFO routing. - Function: Attenuate collisions among S1 workers contributing to one plan. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Overlapping edits, premature dependent work, model-account budget contention, and inconsistent integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which tasks can safely run simultaneously, partition files and change worker/merge commitments when feedback exposes interference. - Decision owner: Model-bearing executor; individual workers respond to revised briefs and correction requests. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Dependency graph, per-task worktrees, assigned owned files, global quota-backed worker cap, agent-link result delivery and merge verification. - Closure path: Plan/worktree overlap identified → executor allocates or delays workers → detects conflict/result → adjusts task/merge order or issues correction → subsequent workers proceed under new ownership. - Boundary reachability: skills/build/executor/SKILL.md is part of the installed standard planner-to-executor flow, and hub spawn/results are first-party tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Deterministic worktree isolation cannot decide semantic independence; the executor is instructed to inspect dependencies and ownership and act on real feedback. - Evidence: executor skill, Step 1 and Step 3, hub.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the documented deployed path, not measured conflict success. - Caveats: No S2 attribution to the FIFO hub alone or the external harnesses&#39; generic team features.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Whole-system current view: The executor reads the complete validated plan, progress ledger, dependency state, quota observations and returned worker results. - Current-control decision scope: Allocate current tasks to workers, choose authorized model routes, adjust concurrency/priority on failures, integrate branches and determine verified completion versus gaps. - Function: Manage present project-plan commitments and recovery across S1 workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Blocked prerequisites, failed results, exhausted accounts, unverified work and integration deviations. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Dispatch independent ready tasks; pause blocked work; choose one bounded retry/correction; integrate accepted changes and decide the plan&#39;s completion report. - Decision owner: Model-bearing executor under the user-approved plan; user holds plan/goal authority outside this S3 autonomous current-control path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Plan ledger, usage observations, worker reports/transcripts, integration branch, validation commands, and limits enforced by router/hub. - Closure path: Current plan and progress → executor observes availability/results → assigns/pauses/repairs commitments → integrates verified code → reports whole-plan status. - Boundary reachability: The default planner handoff opens the executor pane and runs the installed executor protocol; no separate management application needs to implement the decision loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The executor makes substantive current resource/commitment choices; the router, worker cap and test exit codes only constrain/enforce those decisions. - Evidence: executor skill, Steps 0–4, workflow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high for procedural current-control rights, no measured end-to-end success in this review. - Caveats: Parent-approved plan decisions and quotas are not silently reassigned to the executor as S5 governance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Claim being audited: A worker&#39;s reported task completion, validation evidence and claimed changes/commit. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker sends STATUS, summary, validation output, commit and concerns to its parent executor. - Complementary access path: The separate executor independently runs the task&#39;s validation in the worker checkout, checks missing evidence once, reads transcript/state if needed, and returns a bounded correction or records a gap. - Independence boundary: Executor is a separate model-bearing agent from the S1 task owner and checks actual checkout state. It is also S3 current-control owner, not an external third-party auditor; its independent inspection is task-level, not certification of the whole system. - Who acts on findings: The executor requests a correction from the same worker (one retry), otherwise records an unresolved gap, and refuses to integrate an unverified task. - Function: Challenge worker success reports using independent current evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect PASS summaries, absent validation, untested code and integration claims that disagree with actual repository state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Accept, recheck, refuse or return worker work for correction based on direct checkout validation. - Decision owner: The separate model-bearing executor inspecting the S1 worker&#39;s artifacts. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run validation yourself, worktree access, worker brief&#39;s acceptance criteria, one-retry policy, ledger and reporting gaps. - Closure path: Worker claims PASS → executor runs validation against actual worktree → failure or missing evidence returns to worker → corrected artifact is retested or marked a gap before integration. - Boundary reachability: Independent executor check is mandatory in the installed executor skill&#39;s Step 2; the worker and parent run as distinct agent-link sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The command exit code is mechanical, but deciding which claims lack evidence, asking for additional evidence and directing corrections are assigned to the executor; no claim of third-party independence is made. - Evidence: executor skill, Step 2 and Step 3, architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium; independent internal check is mandated, external audit independence is not. - Caveats: Test selection derives from the plan; this is complementary task-work audit, not a proved independent S3 organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - External distinction: Prior completed workflow runs, token/skill-use observations, recurring failure patterns and retained run digests are inputs distinct from current task execution. - Future / prospective distinction: Bounded review generates proposed changes to skills or scoped memory intended to improve later runs, not to repair the current checkout immediately. - Adaptation option generated: Separate scan/review worker suggests concrete before/after changes with supporting/opposing digest evidence, uncertainty and validation. - Path back into current capability / S3: Human reviews an immutable proposal version, approves it explicitly, then the system applies an allowlisted version-checked skill/memory change with validation and receipt; future agent invocations can retrieve/use the approved content. - Function: Parent-authorized development of future harness skills and reusable operational memory from retrospective environment evidence. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Repeated skill misuse, avoidable failed runs and loss of operational experience across future tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Authorize or reject the proposed future change; no automatic model self-modification. - Decision owner: Legitimate local human operator, acting as parent of this local harness instance; scan/review agents propose but do not own approval. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manual /workflow self-learning, separate digest/review workers, scoped target allowlist, version-pinned approval, compare-and-swap apply, validation and receipt. - Closure path: Captured run evidence → agent digest/review proposal → human version-pinned approve → code applies/validates → changed reusable skill or memory becomes available to subsequent S1/S3 tasks. - Boundary reachability: /workflow self-learning, /workflow proposals, /workflow approve and the implementation of version-pinned applyProposal are public built-in commands; this is an operational local parent mode, not a hypothetical external governance overlay. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents make prospective recommendations, but the decisive durable capability choice is explicitly reserved for the human; parent mode is positive without inventing autonomous S4 approval. - Evidence: self-learning.md, learning-review.ts, proposals.ts, workflow.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high for the bounded parent-mode loop; no claim of automatic long-term adaptation quality. - Caveats: Learning is manually invoked and bounded to allowlisted targets; general environmental forecasting and autonomous policy changes are not evidenced.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party owner is evidenced for resolving contested purpose, ultimate policy or identity and returning a legitimate binding organizational decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Account/model authorization and plan ownership are boundaries fixed externally, not internal constitutional disputes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Redefine the system&#39;s binding purpose or policy by an accountable agent or local higher-recursion authority; not supplied. - Decision owner: Human user establishes plan decisions, model/account authorization and local approval of bounded skills; no S5 purpose-adjudication actor within the assessed harness. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Planner user approval, routing allowlists, workflow proposal approvals and limits on remote worker actions. - Closure path: Current plan governance and bounded S4 proposal authorization change operations/capabilities but do not provide an identity/policy question → constitutional authority → binding return loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A human approving a skill patch is not itself a legitimate organizational S5 policy court; the executor cannot override the goal or create new model spending authority. - Evidence: executor skill, self-learning.md, architecture.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high within the reviewed operating modes. - Caveats: A human/project maintainer may exercise external governance; this does not establish a first-party S5 return at the local operational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/49cd03d3257ac0af76d21868ccb44c006f07086c/assessments/pi/pi-house.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 49cd03d3257ac0af76d21868ccb44c006f07086c\">49cd03d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-04T17:47:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yizhi0107-bot/opencode-jarvis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCode Jarvis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-04\">2026-11-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yizhi0107-bot/opencode-jarvis/tree/fb8e47dc6525ec4ff4b4ef333ae7a8d8420c49d7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fb8e47dc6525ec4ff4b4ef333ae7a8d8420c49d7\">fb8e47d &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute code, research, debugging and documentation subtasks through model-bearing specialist workers and tool result feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown files, failing tests, coding defects, unclear task scope and errors returned by tools or delegated roles. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select file modifications, investigations, debug hypotheses, test commands and worker responses within an assigned scoped ticket. - Decision owner: Each specialist LLM role, notably executor/debugger for code writes and architect/scout/scribe for analysis; OpenCode transports actual tool calls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Role-specific tools and permissions, bounded steps, task instructions, acceptance criteria, ticketed subagent launch and test reporting. - Closure path: Parent issues ticket/task → specialist runs model-selected work and observes file/test results → child reports result → parent consumes output and may assign correction. - Boundary reachability: Plugin setup installs the bundled roles via installResources and agent.reload; Jarvis commands and first-party agent prompt delegate to them through the OpenCode host. The claim requires running the declared host, not external maintainer dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker model chooses substantive edits/investigations. The ticket gate restricts scope/parallelism but does not implement the code. - Evidence: executor, debugger, installer, tickets. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high for shipped decision path, medium for actual provider outcome. - Caveats: Models and OpenCode host runtime were not live exercised here; this is a source-level review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Agent-governed adjustment of contemporaneous specialist work to avoid collisions over shared file scopes and tier capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two executor/debugger workers target overlapping files, or local/strong concurrency cap is exhausted while the parent seeks another dispatch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: After refusal, choose to wait, narrow scope, group independent work, change worker or dispatch order, then issue a revised ticket; repeated collision is no longer blindly launched. - Decision owner: Strong-model Jarvis parent chooses changed task boundaries/timing; plugin ticket guard detects and enforces collisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Ticket map with scopeConflict, writesFiles classifier, per-tier caps, dispatch refusal messages, permission hook denying subagents with no issued ticket, completion/TTL cleanup. - Closure path: Active writer ticket creates occupied scope → new overlapping dispatch rejected with actual ticket ID/reason → Jarvis receives refusal and is instructed to revise scope/wait → revised dispatch, or changed sequence, allows nonconflicting work → completion frees scope. - Boundary reachability: src/index.ts registers the governance plugin hooks, and the installed Jarvis role explicitly requires ticketed dispatch and instructs the model to respond to rejection rather than simply repeat it; this is a first-party standard-distribution path. - Distinct S1 units: Named executor and debugger (and other local/strong workers) dispatched as separate model-bearing subagents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Overlapping writer file scopes and excessive simultaneously active workers. - Attenuating coordination relation: Substantive model selection of a revised scope or time coupled to a first-party ticket collision guard. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Returned rejected ticket or completed-ticket audit changes the next dispatch, so subsequent workers run with non-overlapping scopes or deferred timing. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It identifies competing file ownership of two actual worker types and supplies explicit conflict refusal plus returned agent-directed correction; ordinary subagent routing alone would not be credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Collision detection is deterministic, but the living Jarvis model owns the correction and subsequent assignment, making this A in the documented operating mode. - Evidence: tickets collision and issue, tickets hooks, Jarvis discipline, workers. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high for installed per-process tickets. - Caveats: Scope matching is string/path-prefix-based and can miss semantically shared state; it does not prove cross-process or cross-organization conflict isolation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Jarvis allocates and revises current work across the specialized agent portfolio in one managed task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Task difficulty variation, local-model failure, worker resource saturation, poor implementation quality and mismatched work commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decompose task, choose route/tier and worker, set goal/scope/acceptance, decide parallel vs sequential dispatch and escalate executor failures to debugger or reviewer. - Decision owner: Primary strong-model Jarvis in the installed role; human sets initial task and broad resource limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Explicit primary agent with no edit/shell permission, eight worker roster, local/strong tier caps, dispatch ticket lifecycle, route-confidence fallback and scoped result summaries. - Closure path: Current user goal and worker feedback/refusals → Jarvis assesses whole local task and active commitments → changes worker, scope, order or escalation → ticketed child action → returned results for further current decisions. - Boundary reachability: Plugin installs a primary Jarvis agent and dispatch tool, and documented --agent jarvis or TUI selection reaches the agent instructions; /deep assigns a concrete sequence. No independent external PM is required. - Whole-system current view: The supervising Jarvis conversation owns its task decomposition, issued ticket IDs, child returns, failure/rejection feedback and, when requested, aggregated /jarvis-stats telemetry. This is a local one-task composition view, not a cross-session global operations center. - Current-control decision scope: Allocation of current subtask work by model tier, worker, acceptance criteria, file scope, concurrency/ordering and escalation after failure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Jarvis decides substantive current priorities and reassignments; deterministic ticket limits enforce rather than decide the portfolio plan. - Evidence: Jarvis primary role, deep workflow, tickets. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium at declared task recursion; no global whole-project oversight claim. - Caveats: Ticket active map remains internal to one plugin process; operational view is parent conversation and its task, not all users or projects.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Separate read-only reviewer role independently checks implemented code and tests, returning repair findings to the task controller. - Disturbance / variety regulated: An executor may claim a successful change despite defects, regression risks, boundary gaps or insufficient tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect real files/test outcomes distinct from executor narrative, judge severity and return issues for correcting the actual implementation. - Decision owner: Jarvis/reviewer model, separate from writer executor and constrained to read/grep/glob plus user-confirmed shell tests; Jarvis orders returned corrections. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Reviewer role permission boundary denying edits, dedicated reviewerModel option, /deep mandated review and severity-triggered executor fix + one re-review, /council independent judge inputs. - Closure path: Executor reports implemented diff → separate reviewer reads working source and may run checks → reviewer returns high/medium findings with file:line/severity → Jarvis sends executor correction → reviewer rechecks, then final report. - Boundary reachability: Bundled reviewer and /deep templates are installed in global/project scope by the first-party installer; the exact installed command explicitly mandates reviewer and returned fix/review. No external CI auditor is borrowed. - Claim being audited: Correctness, regressions, boundaries, maintainability and test evidence for this task&#39;s implementation. - Ordinary reporting path: Executor&#39;s changed-file and verification report to Jarvis after its assigned work. - Complementary access path: Reviewer uses independent tool calls to read/search changed files and, with permission, run tests/static checks. - Independence boundary: Separate model role and tool restrictions (read-only; cannot edit); reviewer does not merely repeat executor&#39;s output, but its subprocess still shares the workspace and is not an organizationally independent external auditor. - Who acts on findings: Jarvis directs executor to repair high/medium issues and orders one subsequent reviewer pass in /deep mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewing model makes its own issue/severity judgment from code; controller/host pass its findings to an explicit correction path. - Evidence: reviewer role, deep workflow, council workflow. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium for documented correction ownership; direct provider-level independence and efficacy not runtime tested. - Caveats: A bundled command instructing review is not itself evidence of actual effective audit in every provider run. CLI must explicitly select --agent jarvis for the /deep pathway, as README warns.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No installed prospective external environment sensing and future system capability adaptation loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Cost/quality routing, output limits and failed tools are handled for the present request. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Form and select a strategic future adaptation option and return it to current capability governance; not supplied. - Decision owner: Jarvis selects current task route; operator/maintainer changes models/options and versions outside a first-party prospective policy cycle. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Triage confidence fallback, model availability warning, metrics audit and /jarvis-stats historical aggregation, setup/reload command. - Closure path: Metrics are visible to a user and routing decisions affect present task; no automatic or mandatory future adaptation selection and capability-return channel is closed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Cheap-vs-strong model choice is an immediate current allocation (S3), not automatically outside-and-then strategic intelligence. - Evidence: Jarvis prompt, commands/stats, options. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Human may analyze historical routing metrics and change configuration manually; this is not a separately closed S4 mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separate ultimate identity or policy adjudication with authoritative returned governance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unauthorized dispatch and unsafe commands are bounded by fixed plugin ticket and role permission rules. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Resolve a continuing identity/ultimate-policy conflict and issue binding policy for later organizational operations; absent. - Decision owner: Maintainer-designed Jarvis purpose and role templates, human-selected installation scope/models; tickets enforce operational rules rather than a changing S5 mandate. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: User configuration, agentScope global/project/off, role permissions, no-ticket dispatch refusal, selected local/strong models. - Closure path: User selects options or model and reloads; call gates execute configured rules. No S5 identity question → legitimate ultimate authority judgment → binding policy-return feedback loop is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Fixed local-first identity and per-call permission policies do not create self-governing S5; config changes alone do not establish P. - Evidence: options, installer, tickets. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: OpenCode&#39;s broader host or a user&#39;s project governance may supply policy elsewhere but is not this plugin&#39;s credited S5.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/07514974531a8af0ddc884c15695b06084b7ab0b/assessments/op/opencode-jarvis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 07514974531a8af0ddc884c15695b06084b7ab0b\">0751497 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T20:51:25+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewiKim/autoresearchlab\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoResearchLab</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TaewiKim/autoresearchlab/tree/bd70a67aafa172a4e7c5a3f4477d6e438cfeb0d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision bd70a67aafa172a4e7c5a3f4477d6e438cfeb0d8\">bd70a67 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Autonomous role lanes produce scoped scientific work: locate and evaluate evidence, design a falsifiable experiment, provision execution, run analysis, and write defensible results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unfamiliar academic domains, unavailable tools/datasets, conflicting data quality, unanticipated engineering failures, analytical alternatives and result uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Scout agents choose/refine field-specific queries, Analyst chooses estimands and tests, Engineer chooses how to provision the selected toolchain without altering the science, and Runner writes/runs the contract-bound analysis; their outputs determine subsequent experimental work. - Decision owner: Configured model-backed role agents operating through the first-party lane contracts; external Codex CLI executes the model sessions but its mere availability does not establish additional first-party S-functions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust lane dispatch, role-scoped sandboxes, sealed contracts/artifacts, runtime state and deterministic validity checks. - Closure path: Role plan → agent work/result artifact → contract and gate checks → next role uses the output or reports a blocker to the director → subsequent experiment/review changes accordingly. - Boundary reachability: The shipped CLI and agent plan construct these Codex role lanes, the native harness runs them and persists returned results; these are not example-only or CI-only role stubs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents possess bounded scientific and implementation discretion; queue/budget enforcement and filesystem persistence are deterministic supports, not the source of their scientific choices. - Evidence: role rights; reasoning roles in shipped plan; native lane entry. - Basis: Structural shipped agent-role/decision and result-return paths, supplemented by explicit source-owning architecture descriptions. - Confidence: High for first-party configurability and decision ownership, not a measurement of model effectiveness. - Caveats: No live Codex/LLM agent, scholarly API or actual research experiment was run in this assessment; science quality is not a VSM autonomy grade.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate stabilization of cross-role experiment/review work, but a separately established inter-S1 oscillation-attenuation function remains ambiguous at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing author experiment requests, engineering/analysis handoff dependencies, status messages crowding other channels and duplicate/superseded work may disrupt delivery to other scientific units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The bus carries author→director requests and enforces per-channel capacity, unique identity, delivery receipts and escalation; LabDirector can adopt/decline experiments with reasons. It is not established that the latter is a distinct inter-S1 damping decision rather than S3 prioritization and sequential task handoff. - Decision owner: Agents answer substantive requests, but deterministic bus/scheduler implements capacity, escalation and delivery; S2-specific ownership is unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Request/standing channel registry, per-channel quotas, stateful deduplication, escalations, lane sequencing and contract-bound handoffs. - Closure path: Author request → bus → director response → changed experiment/document plan is plausible, but the reviewed evidence does not separate this from hierarchical current-control return sufficiently for an autonomous S2 claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual feedback messages and decisions exist; their S2-specific function and direct reciprocal stabilization of distinct operational units cannot be inferred merely from those mechanisms. - Evidence: author request with owed reason; per-channel capacity and delivery; projection and return; documented channel history. - Basis: Structural coordination candidates; unknown function-specific inter-S1 relation. - Confidence: Medium that coordination machinery exists; low on attribution of a separate S2 function. - Caveats: The specific unresolved witness is two independently operating S1 cells whose mutual interference, rather than a parent backlog/approval dependency, is attenuated through an accountable, closed S2 path.\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate present scientific commitments and next work for the entire current research program, responding to failed reviews, stalled runs, evidence gaps and competing follow-up demands. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Expensive repeated analyses, weak lead hypotheses, unresolved gate blockers, scientific stagnation, contradictory handoffs and limited lane budget/time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: LabDirector selects/refines/pivots/concludes a research lead and chooses follow-up scientific tasks, based on Chronicle, current gate results and supervisor notices; it may not override independently recorded reviewer scores. - Decision owner: The model-backed LabDirector agent for scientific priority/correction. Supervisor stall detection, Rust dispatch/termination, budgets and hard rubric gates constrain or request action but do not own that scientific decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Cycle-wide summaries and notes, request escalations, lane priority/dispatch, budget and stagnation guards, protected lead memory, append-only feedback. - Closure path: Whole-cycle evidence/active review blocker → director&#39;s scientific correction/pivot/priority decision → role task/lead updates → scheduler launches affected subsequent lanes → newer results are reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: The default agent plan includes a LabDirector across stages, and the native cycle uses its lead decisions and reviewer feedback to create follow-ups; not a maintainer-only or external governance workflow. - Whole-system current view: Chronicle digest, evidence/decision notes, active blockers, gate review and lane/cycle states across the same research-program boundary. - Current-control decision scope: Next research work, which lead/claim is current, which further measurements or corrections are owed, and whether to pivot or conclude under the fixed scientific and resource constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The director has substantive authority to adjudicate scientific commitments; a deterministic forced-stall request cannot replace its selection of a scientifically justified next hypothesis. - Evidence: director role and limits; agent plan duty; active blocker review return; source-bound strategy decisions. - Basis: Explicit role contract plus structural gate→decision→followup path. - Confidence: Medium-high for source-wired current control; no live effectiveness evidence. - Caveats: Forced livelock termination and resource enforcement are deterministic. Owner instructions can constrain individual choices, but ordinary operator steering does not by itself establish a distinct closed S3 parent mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently challenge the producing research roles&#39; hypothesis, validity, technical/reproducibility and reporting claims before acceptance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Novelty overclaims, unsound analysis, stale evidence, unreplicated results, and a production lane&#39;s optimistic interpretation of its own outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Separately dispatched Gatekeeper model agents assess current bound evidence and return approve/challenge/reject, rubric scores and missing validations; their judgments cannot be overwritten by LabDirector. - Decision owner: Independent Gatekeeper agent lanes; deterministic rubric parsing, conjunctive gates and scheduling enforce the returned review, without claiming autonomous judgment for the scorer code itself. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate lane state, current-lead/validation binding, reviewer role split, sealed panel reports, structural rubric gates and append-only review history. - Closure path: Producing roles&#39; claim and validation artifacts → separate reviewer access/judgment → gate objections and rejection persisted → LabDirector receives binding scientific blockers and chooses changed investigation/followup → subsequent review of revised evidence. - Boundary reachability: Gatekeepers are included in the normal agent plan and their result parsing/aggregation is wired into the production review path; this is not CI testing or a developer-only quality script. - Claim being audited: Research lead&#39;s purported novelty, technical validity, reproducibility, measured results and completeness of evidence. - Ordinary reporting path: Analyst/Runner outputs and LabDirector/Chronicle research summary. - Complementary access path: Separate gatekeeper prompts bound to the current lead and validation artifacts, literature graph and prior reviews; independent rubric outputs and objections. - Independence boundary: Distinct role/lanes, sealed review collection and prohibition on director score override, though all may use the same external model family; independence is organizational/role, not proof of model independence or external peer review. - Who acts on findings: LabDirector for scientific replanning; native gate enforces publication/promotion hold until sufficient review. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-backed reviewers choose scientific objections and verdicts with their own role contracts. The JS aggregator applies those decisions but does not generate the underlying audit judgment. - Evidence: separation of powers; independent lane design; gatekeeper plan; review parsing and provenance; review objections/return. - Basis: Explicit source boundary plus executable reviewer-lane/result-return structure. - Confidence: Medium-high on first-party complementary audit mode, not on external peer-review quality. - Caveats: A Gatekeeper may see summaries rather than independently replaying an experiment; S3 positive is narrowly complementary access through separate role, literature/review evidence and scientific corrective return, not a claim of adversarial execution of the raw dataset on every run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: Model a changing scientific field and generate future research-agenda options from its beliefs, gaps and frontier, returning selected options into the current program&#39;s experimental capabilities. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New competing hypotheses and publications, oversaturated or already-solved research questions, uncertainty about future impact and which experimental directions deserve commitment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Agent-owned Scout/LabDirector field/belief/candidate synthesis and independent expert comparisons develop future-facing research options. A supported timeout mode automatically promotes the highest eligible ranked survivor; a distinct normal mode lets the researcher choose from the same evidence-grounded candidate slate, after which LabDirector operationalizes the choice. - Decision owner: Autonomous research agents own the external interpretation, slate and expert judgments in the base mode; the legitimate cycle-owning human explicitly owns the final agenda choice in the supported selection mode. Rust gate merely enforces the chosen candidate and resumes work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Field landscape quality gate, SOTA cards, instrument-blind candidate slate, sealed expert panel, novelty refutation, feasibility anchors, owner pause/selection file and timeout policy. - Closure path: Field literature/frontier → SOTA/beliefs → agent-created candidate futures → panel and novelty/feasibility filtering → automatic survivor or parent-selected candidate → LabDirector lead and reproduction anchor → current Analyst/Engineer/Runner work changes. - Boundary reachability: Expert selection is dispatched by the first-party native program when a lead is absent and its landscape/cards are ready; the published owner pause and timeout are supported runtime paths, not research-paper suggestions. - External distinction: Newly examined scientific claims, field-wide paper frontiers, contradictions, open problems and external assets in the requested research field. - Future / prospective distinction: A ranked set of not-yet-undertaken research programs designed to change accepted beliefs, evaluated for novelty, uncertainty and feasible reproduction. - Adaptation option generated: Alternative research hypotheses and experiment programs, including cross-field/bottleneck paths; the selected candidate changes the research lab&#39;s lead and required future experiment capabilities. - Path back into current capability / S3: The selected-vetted candidate is supplied as the bound new lead and next reproduction work in the director&#39;s current-control path, constraining subsequent role task creation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agent roles make the field/option judgments, with an operational automatic option; separately the user has a genuine alternative supported decision right, so (P) is not inferred merely from a configuration knob. - Evidence: source landscape selection engagement; instrument-blind slate; owner choice return to lead; runtime owner checkpoint; timeout selection. - Basis: Structural source paths for field-informed future agenda and two alternative supported selection owners. - Confidence: Medium for S4 ownership boundary; high for the existence of selection paths. - Caveats: This is prospective adaptation of a research program&#39;s agenda, not proven autonomous redesign of AutoResearchLab&#39;s own software, model weights or deployment. Whether the optional timeout is enabled is configuration-dependent; no live literature search or model evaluation was run.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No separately established first-party ultimate identity/policy arbitration with a closed decision-return path at the research-program recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User research directions and binding owner directives affect venue, method count, project preferences and evidentiary discipline, but these are program/task priorities and source-quality constraints rather than a demonstrated ultimate-policy dispute. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The owner may submit a research request, select the lead, and record binding instructions with reasons. No shipped role or explicit parent path adjudicates a conflict over the institution&#39;s identity/ultimate policy as distinct from research-agenda/current-control decisions. - Decision owner: External research owner supplies task/domain/program preferences; validity controls enforce them without acquiring ultimate-policy discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Research request/configuration, owner-directive file, role prompt injection, scientific score rules and selected-candidate enforcement. - Closure path: Owner research instructions → role task constraints and agenda decisions. Missing identity-level issue → legitimate policy deliberation/decision → governing policy returned to subsequent program operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A binding preference and research agenda selection are material parent decisions, but not automatically S5. A static instruction or scientific gate also cannot own ultimate policy merely by enforcement. - Evidence: owner directives and scope; binding role clause; owner research selection. - Basis: Bounded source-first negative after distinguishing parent agenda and scientific control from true ultimate-policy authority. - Confidence: Medium-high within the supported single research-program boundary. - Caveats: A human university or research institution can exercise a higher-recursion S5 outside the software; no organizational parent mode is imported from its mere possible presence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8610c1721397b1fd22b2b433c18fbc1e01311151/assessments/au/autoresearchlab.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8610c1721397b1fd22b2b433c18fbc1e01311151\">8610c17 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T21:32:05+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VellocLabs/velloc-runtime\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Velloc Runtime</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/VellocLabs/velloc-runtime/tree/28bccb9a03b696efc1890c4b12cb1366646d975c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 28bccb9a03b696efc1890c4b12cb1366646d975c\">28bccb9 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute coding/user tasks by selecting tools, editing artifacts and reacting to tool, model and delegated results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing source state, task ambiguity, model/tool errors, missing context, time and token budgets. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose action/tool, agent delegation, implementation revision or final result. - Decision owner: Active session model; child or teammate models own their bounded delegated task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Turn reducer/interpreter, tool contracts, permission gates, model drivers, session ledger, contexts and host capabilities. - Closure path: Conversation and repository context → model request → tool/subagent decision → executed effect and observation → next turn or output. - Boundary reachability: The host loads the first-party runtime, whose session worker invokes the bundled engine; named built-in Explore, Plan and general-purpose agent routes are compiled into the shipped tool catalog. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Model-generated coding and tool decisions respond to execution feedback; the deterministic engine only transports and enforces permissions. - Evidence: runtime overview, turn engine, built-in agents, tool registry. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Frozen source inspection only; no live host/model session was executed by this assessment.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate collisions and incompatible sequencing between concurrently working code agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple workers changing overlapping files or acting before prerequisites complete, stale tasks and ambiguous ownership. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Split nonoverlapping scopes, assign/claim dependency-unblocked tasks, isolate writable worktrees and return conflicts for revised allocation. - Decision owner: Team lead model following first-party agent-teams/batch protocols, with teammates updating board ownership and completion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskCreate/TaskUpdate/TaskList, SendMessage, task blocks/blockedBy, long-lived teammates, worktree isolation and per-member locks/IDs. - Closure path: Lead defines slices/dependencies → owner claims eligible work → agents execute in allocated locations → board/reports expose blocked/completed or overlapping work → lead updates assignment or integration plan. - Boundary reachability: The shipped agent-teams hosted skill unlocks team tools, while the bundled /batch skill is registered by the default stock-skill installer for compatible sessions; neither requires a third-party coordinator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead and members select scope and assignments using live shared task state. The registry stores decisions but does not autonomously decide them. - Distinct S1 units: Lead and separately running teammate/Agent worker models, each executing independent assigned code work in its own session/worktree. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Concurrent writers can collide in the same source tree; a teammate may take work blocked by another; unowned or stale tasks create duplicated work. - Attenuating coordination relation: Shared owner/dependency board plus scoped worktree assignment and lead instruction to avoid overlapping slices; one worker claims eligible unblocked tasks, others take a different unit. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Task completion, ownership and blockers are returned through TaskList and mailbox reports, allowing the lead/teammates to claim the next unblocked job or revise work assignments. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: It actively regulates conflicting edit scope and work eligibility between multiple independently acting S1 units, using ownership and isolated write locations with returned task-status feedback. - Evidence: agent teams instruction and gate, team board state, model-facing task tools, worktree-based batch. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: S2 is credited to user-activated team/stock-skill modes; no implicit inter-session management across every Velloc chat is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Regulate current multi-worker commitment, execution progress and integration against the common coding goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Worker failure, unowned or blocked tasks, results that fail validation, privilege exposure and integration readiness. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead chooses next assignments, requests fixes, halts unsafe work and accepts/integrates results based on task-board and current artifact evidence. - Decision owner: Team lead model in bundled team/batch/safe-team modes, distinct from delegated implementation models. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team roster, mutable task board, automatic final-report/message turns, Shutdown, host tool permission gates, stable candidate snapshots and lead-owned validation. - Closure path: Team&#39;s live task/output state → lead assesses current readiness/security → changes assignments, requests corrections or stops the worker → updated work state returns for acceptance. - Boundary reachability: agent-teams exposes tools for lead and worker progress updates; bundled /safe-team instructs a trusted lead to review a frozen candidate and route actual validation failures back to teammates. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The whole-task lead exercises discretionary management on a multi-worker whole rather than merely following a fixed per-tool policy. - Whole-system current view: The lead reads the team&#39;s complete task list, owners/dependencies, teammate status and current candidate artifacts/validation output. - Current-control decision scope: The lead can adjust assignments/priorities, request fresh fixes, stop unsafe writers, hold integration and authorize acceptance of the reviewed result for the current team task. - Evidence: team skill, task/teammate tools, safe-team current management, teammate reporting. - Basis: structural and explicit. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: This is conditional team-level S3, not evidence of central capacity management across all installed users/sessions or proof of real-world success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently examine a code change for actual defects and return findings for author repair. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Developer self-confirmation, missed reuse/design/performance defects and unsafe changes from untrusted workers. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Fresh reviewer agents report specific file/line faults; the author/lead triages and fixes or rejects them based on observed code. - Decision owner: Three separately spawned review-model actors in bundled simplify mode; in safe-team a trusted lead retains independent snapshot/security acceptance rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Captured git diff, independent reviewer briefs for reuse/design/performance, read-only reviewer roles, source-level findings, corrections and stable-snapshot inspection. - Closure path: Author&#39;s changed diff → separately briefed reviewer agents inspect raw code/neighbours → file/line findings → author merges/deduplicates and edits fixes → resulting artifact is returned to normal task validation. - Boundary reachability: installStockSkills registers simplify by default for compatible agent/code sessions with the native Agent tool; team-only safe-team is another shipped optional complementary path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Review models independently examine the current diff and issue discretionary findings, not merely restate the author&#39;s report or run deterministic tests. - Claim being audited: Change quality and safety against the task and existing project code. - Ordinary reporting path: The implementing model&#39;s changes and own summary. - Complementary access path: Fresh reviewers read raw git diff and surrounding repository implementation, with distinct review briefs. - Independence boundary: Separate spawned reviewer model sessions/roles from author execution; same underlying provider may be used; no independent institutional auditor claimed. - Who acts on findings: Original author model fixes findings, drops unsupported ones with reasons, and validates the result; trusted lead approves or halts untrusted code acceptance in safe-team mode. - Evidence: bundled simplify review loop, stock skill wiring, safe-team independent check, native agent dispatch. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: User-invoked or model-invoked skill mode, not mandatory audit of every change; review accuracy or an enforced second audit round is not asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: No organization-level outside/future intelligence and capability adaptation loop demonstrated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Evolving external technology and future organizational fitness demands. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separately established prospective option authority; user or developer installs plugins and selects models. - Decision owner: No qualifying S4 agent identified. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scheduled routines, cross-session memory extraction/consolidation, model profile switching, extension/plugin marketplaces and developer-facing updating tools. - Closure path: Memory can be recalled and routines scheduled; no clearly owned external-future sensing → strategic option → implemented capability-change loop is returned to the task system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reorganizing prior session notes and user-configured provider changes support continuity and current tasks, not prospective environment-driven adaptation on their own. - Evidence: memory consolidation, routine scheduler, runtime overview. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A user-written task could ask an agent to research future plans, but that is not a separately shipped governance mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No ultimate identity/legitimacy dispute arbitration and governing return established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting ultimate goals, authority claims and definition of permissible system identity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No governing S5 final decision process observed within the runtime boundary. - Decision owner: Operator/host chooses capabilities, model trust and permissions; team lead controls task security, not ultimate constitutional authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Host-granted versioned capabilities, permission modes, protected paths, managed-over-user mod tiers and provider trust settings. - Closure path: Static operator policy is enforced by permission checks; no identity dispute is escalated to final authority and returned as a redefined governing policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: safe-team is security/current-work supervision; an app-level permission envelope and developer governance are not an operational S5 function by mere presence. - Evidence: runtime capability boundary, safe-team, team provider trust. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: No parent organizational constitution or owner-level ultimate-policy decision should be inferred from default safety settings.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/67f1f884467852ba81d9cbcd153c79c85cd89666/assessments/ve/velloc-runtime.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 67f1f884467852ba81d9cbcd153c79c85cd89666\">67f1f88 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-17T22:33:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/blacsheep/agt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">agt</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-17\">2026-11-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/blacsheep/agt/tree/14ff89edc8f4f3c9e7be2c45ae11c903a794f5f0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 14ff89edc8f4f3c9e7be2c45ae11c903a794f5f0\">14ff89e &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Deliver open-ended user goals by model-backed agents making task, message and tool choices in supported chats. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unpredictable user requirements, tool outcomes, teammates&#39; results and incomplete tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An active model-backed lead or specialist decides whom to message, which tool to use, what work to attempt and how to follow up. - Decision owner: First-party configured model loop for lead and team members; remote coding-harness adapters supply optional S1 execution but do not own the native lead&#39;s project choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent-bound resource tools, MCP, sessions, scheduler, SQLite and approval gates. - Closure path: User goal → model tool calls/task delegation → worker result → model follow-up or report in initiating conversation. - Boundary reachability: CLI/Web quick start creates a lead with shipped prompt, resourcemanagement and taskcollaboration; native model loop is supported. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model decides substantive next investigative/team actions; deterministic transport/permission does not decide the content. - Evidence: lead setup; tracked task lifecycle; native engine - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate real contention when independent team agents might work on the same resources and duplicate or overwrite work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Simultaneous tasks sharing named exclusive resources, duplicate requests and race conditions between agents editing common artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead agent selects assignees, task priority and claimed resources, and revises queue/dependencies after blockers or conflicting outcomes. - Decision owner: Model-backed lead in supported mode owns the coordination choice; scheduler enforces declared resource exclusion, revision guards and idempotent request keys. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Persistent tasks, exclusiveresources, revision CAS, queue guard/aging and scheduler concurrency limits. - Closure path: Agent task claims → overlap/unknown outcome blocks other claims → lead sees task state and reports → adjusts assignee/resource/queue, and scheduler applies new admissible work. - Boundary reachability: First-party taskcollaboration manager available to configured lead; optional scheduler.parallelwork plus documented exclusive resources. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Scheduler does not pick which conflicting business objective wins; lead&#39;s model-directed task revisions and choices retain discretion, bounded by deterministic exclusion. - Distinct S1 units: Lead and multiple independent worker/research/coding agents with separate execution sessions and bound resources. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Two tasks can touch the same named project files/operations concurrently and could overwrite each other; interrupted outcomes must not be retried while resources are held. - Attenuating coordination relation: First-party task exclusiveresources block overlapping work; lead-managed priorities, dependencies and outcomeunknown resolution change future dispatch. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Blocking states, task reports and revisioned queue changes determine which worker is admitted to execute afterward. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The code and docs specify explicit shared-resource mutual exclusion, uncertain-outcome fences and conflict-aware deferred work, not merely a mailbox. - Evidence: resource exclusion and outcomeunknown; queue/CAS; scheduler fencing; lead delegation - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Manage the team&#39;s current work commitments, agent roster and blocked assignments across one local workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Current task backlog, stalled outcomes, disabled workers, urgency changes, pending returns and finite concurrent slots. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Lead inspects current agents/tasks and reassigns, reprioritizes, cancels or creates work and restores progress; workspace owner has a distinct dashboard/CLI management mode. - Decision owner: Base: model-backed lead with task and resource-management capabilities. Parent mode: human workspace owner with Web Tasks/Agent team current controls. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Scheduler, goals, task state, hard reply/time limits, team listing and persistent conversations. - Closure path: Team/task/goal state and returned worker messages → lead or owner makes present assignment/intervention → tools and scheduler change who works next. - Boundary reachability: Bundled lead prompt and task/resource tools; owner console displays all team work and exposes queue/agent stop/retry/reassign options. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Lead is an actual model participant deciding task portfolio; human&#39;s separate current-operations mode does not turn every lead choice into parent governance. - Whole-system current view: Task queue, agent roster, goals, live conversation statuses and pending approvals across the local workspace. - Current-control decision scope: Current work priority, responsible agent/team membership, resumption/stop and resolution of unknown outcomes. - Evidence: lead agency; goal and current queue control; goal watcher; scheduler limits - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No installed claim-relative independent review of actual ordinary team operations is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Specialists can make faulty claims; independent observation of their primary artifacts would be needed for complementary operational audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate installed audit owner proved to receive operational evidence and feed corrections back to the lead. - Decision owner: Not established within the base lead/team standard distribution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional model permission reviewer examines contemplated tool arguments; user can create a critic agent in a research group; scheduler records traces. - Closure path: Permission review gates future action but does not independently inspect completed team output; example critic receives reports, not a proved complementary primary-artifact audit path. - Boundary reachability: Reviewer mode is a first-party permission feature, but is not an operational-audit path merely by being separate. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prospective tool authorization and report-only debate do not establish S3; no first-party audit-then-return loop tied to actual S1 production found. - Evidence: tool authorization review; team critic example; result reports - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: Change the future team&#39;s capabilities when requirements expose missing roles, tools or integrations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A new task demands expertise, API connectors or isolated tooling that the current team lacks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The model-backed lead decides which agent roles and new tool capabilities are needed, commissions a coding harness, tests/registers the new MCP and binds it to future workers. - Decision owner: First-party lead model in the documented dynamic-team mode; coder model can create code, while the lead determines and activates the capacity change within permission envelope. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Resource management tools for prompts/models/agents/MCPs, remote coding harness runner, tool probing, named permissions and persistent resource files. - Closure path: Future-relevant task/tool gap → lead&#39;s adaptation plan → coded/probed MCP and new agent role → registered/granted to the team → subsequent work uses the new capability. - Boundary reachability: Shipped lead prompt explicitly teaches discover/reuse/build/test/bind on ordinary user goals; resourcemanagement and taskcollaboration are first-party built-ins. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead interprets the unmet requirement and chooses new capabilities; file persistence and API checks are supporting mechanisms, not the adaptation decision maker. - External distinction: Incoming goal&#39;s external requirements and newly encountered absent integration relative to currently listed models/agents/tools. - Future / prospective distinction: The team prepares reusable capabilities and role definitions for subsequent work rather than only completing the present atomic tool call. - Adaptation option generated: Agent role or new MCP service, with model-directed test, registration and per-agent binding. - Path back into current capability / S3: The lead changes the workspace agent/tool inventory, and subsequent scheduler-dispatched roles can execute the new configured tool. - Evidence: first-party toolsmith adaptation; resource-managed teams; builtin tool registry - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No first-party ultimate-identity conflict escalation/ratification loop shown for the local agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Identity, constitutional purpose and standing limits could require an ultimate choice outside task-level permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No qualifying first-party authority forum/owner and return-to-operation path for fundamental policy disputes shown. - Decision owner: Human workspace owner controls standing permissions and agent prompts, but no S5 closure beyond ordinary authorization is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-tool approval, permission policies, lead bootstrap instructions, resource identities and user stop controls. - Closure path: The runtime enforces configured permission decisions on tool operations; there is no evidenced identity-level tension/ratification and returned constitutional change. - Boundary reachability: Human controls are installed but configure/approve current actions rather than run a policy-identity deliberation path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead can create roles and tools, but changing a task roster or requiring user approval is not by itself an S5 constitutional decision. - Evidence: lead and owner limits; approval boundary; operating constraints - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/47f000a384998ed7fdf69827e592d25224581e55/assessments/ag/agt.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 47f000a384998ed7fdf69827e592d25224581e55\">47f000a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-02T17:04:06+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/roloVibes/Halo-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Halo Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-02\">2026-11-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/roloVibes/Halo-Harness/tree/710dde651035be3f204598aee90a1e4b85e3d6e8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 710dde651035be3f204598aee90a1e4b85e3d6e8\">710dde6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute model-directed workspace tasks and operational worker lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain code/research work, tool failures, provider/context limits and incomplete artifacts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose next tool, code/research action or response from feedback. - Decision owner: The first-party session/worker model actors. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Python model/tool loop, provider transports, permission engine, local tools/MCP, sessions, role lineups and worktrees. - Closure path: Model request → selected tools → observations/session state → subsequent model step or worker result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Halo owns its loop rather than merely configuring Claude Code. Claude-compatible settings and external model endpoints do not donate control functions. - Evidence: haloharness/agent/loop.py; haloharness/agent/subagent.py; haloharness/orchestra/workers.py; README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Normal halo CLI/TUI and Orchestra adapters instantiate the native session/worker loop; installed role/model configuration does not require a third-party agent runtime.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Regulate overlapping file-unit writes and merge conflicts across distinct Orchestra worker lanes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agents may claim overlapping functions/file regions or produce mutually conflicting changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Workers choose a different claim, wait or escalate on refusal; a fresh judge selects a conflict resolution when branches collide. - Decision owner: Worker/lead model actors and the fresh conflict-judge model own the discretionary choices; code owns atomic admission and merge/test enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AST/heading/file-unit claim keys, exclusive claim log, guarded writes, per-lane worktrees, serialized merges, test checks and bounded send-back. - Closure path: Claim refusal returns holder/expiry to worker → changed claim/wait/escalation; conflicting branches → fresh judge sees goal/hunks → proposed text applied/tested → failed resolution undone and piece returned to the worker for revision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A is grounded in model-owned response to a concrete cross-S1 disturbance, particularly the fresh conflict resolution and worker correction, not in deterministic locks or sequence alone. - Evidence: docs/ORCHESTRA.md; haloharness/orchestra/claims.py; haloharness/tools/orchestraclaims.py; haloharness/orchestra/merge.py; haloharness/orchestra/adapters.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Orchestra run creates real native worker sessions and exposes claim tools/guard in their run environment; merge uses the shipped fresh-judge adapter.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate whole-run current resources and commitments after forecast/return/block/stall drift. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Projected overspend, repeated returns, blocked dependencies/claims and stalled lanes threaten the current goal organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose revised model/bio, split remaining work, add a read-only specialist, retry with corrective context or stop; in review mode the owner decides whether to apply the proposed revision. - Decision owner: Auto mode: the lead model owns the checked replan. Review/parked budget mode: the legitimate local operator owns the returned acceptance/cap decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Drift thresholds, current lane/spend/forecast snapshots, plan validation, immutable merged-lane protection, pause/checkpoint, durable decision rows and deterministic apply/resume. - Closure path: Drift → lanes pause → lead receives whole remaining plan/current evidence → auto applies a valid agent plan, or review parks its diff → operator approves/rejects/adjusts cap → split/claims/workflow/forecast updated and remaining operation resumes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The lead makes the substantive current-control revision in auto mode; validation and budgets enforce it. Review mode separately returns an operator decision into operation. Neither hard stops nor initial task splitting alone supplies the positive witness. - Evidence: docs/SUPERVISE.md; haloharness/supervise/drift.py; haloharness/supervise/replanask.py; haloharness/supervise/replanapply.py; haloharness/supervise/decisions.py; haloharness/supervise/apply.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Optional supervised modes; replan limits/cooldown apply, completed lanes are pinned, workflow-pinned stage sets disable free replanning, and budget ceilings remain separately operator-owned.. - Boundary reachability: halo supervise/goal options expose replan:auto and replan:review; ordinary runtime adapters call the lead and apply checked plans. Attention answers are polled and applied to the same queued goal.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Complement production claims with raw-code multi-dimension review, independent finding adjudication and correction/re-verification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Author summaries or reviewer allegations may be wrong; a claimed fix may leave the defect intact. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reviewer finds code defects; independent judge confirms/dismisses against source; confirmed findings are corrected by a separate fixer and judged again against the new code. - Decision owner: Fresh read-only reviewer/judge model actors own audit judgments; a coder actor owns scoped correction. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Built-in dimension prompts, fixed target/diff resolution, read-only agent specs omitting CLAUDE.md, structured verdicts, serial fixes, file hashes and current-source re-verification. - Closure path: halo review target --fix → raw artifact review → judge traces claim → CONFIRMED finding goes to fixer → changed file hash/current diff → judge rechecks → fixed only when the defect is dismissed; failed/unparseable checks remain PLAUSIBLE/skipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The separate code-inspecting actors add complementary reality access and a shipped corrective return. Deterministic Orchestra gates and model tuning statistics are not promoted to audit ownership. - Evidence: docs/REVIEW.md; haloharness/review/run.py; haloharness/review/agents.py; haloharness/review/verify.py; haloharness/review/fix.py; haloharness/review/prompts/judge.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Report-only mode alone is not claimed to complete correction. The positive witness includes --fix with verification; review exit code 0 is not evidence of zero findings or approval.. - Boundary reachability: halo review and /review call the common built-in runreview; --fix is a shipped option, not an unwired developer callback, and it requires verification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Develop prospective model-roster capability adaptations from external ecosystem options and local operating constraints, with operator selection returned into the roster/roles. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Missing embedding/vision/role capabilities, model-family blind-spot overlap and newly available model families can leave future tasks unsupported or costly. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose whether to add/verify a new external model, retire an overlap or repoint a role after inspecting capability/cost/fit options. - Decision owner: The legitimate self-hosting operator; deterministic adviser/battery/UI supply options and enforcement, not an autonomous S4 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: External Ollama/Hugging Face library/catalog metadata and dated snapshot, local host memory/capability profiles, gap/freshness/mismatch rules, ranked options with download/VRAM/role impact, download-and-verify job and explicit role acceptance. - Closure path: External/local roster distinctions → bounded ranked adaptation options → operator selects Download and verify/retire/point → model pulled, battery profile written and role offered → accepted assignment updates the active lineup/role for subsequent work. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: P is supported by a function-specific compare/select/apply path. Neither deterministic recommendation nor ordinary current model routing is called autonomous S4; the operator owns the prospective capability choice. - Evidence: docs/MODELS-adviser.md; docs/MODELS-ollama.md; haloharness/modelslibrary/advise.py; haloharness/modelslibrary/adviserules.py; haloharness/modelslibrary/browse.py; haloharness/studio/localsearchapi.py; haloharness/studio/localverifyjob.py; haloharness/studio/rolespointapi.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: The adviser is deterministic and the packaged ecosystem snapshot can be stale; freshness is not measured superiority. No live download, benchmark or successful capability gain is claimed by this source assessment.. - Boundary reachability: Official halo local advise/Studio Local Models/Library exposes suggestions and the same supported verify/assign/role-point routes; this is installed product behavior, not a research plan.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No identity/ultimate-policy arbitration function is supplied at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Goal stop criteria, permissions, fleet control grants, role bios and deployment policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No function-specific identity/ultimate-policy decisive return path established. - Decision owner: Operator/configuration owns constraints; no qualifying S5 owner/path established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: WHAT/DONE/STOP goal fields, role/lineup configuration, approval and fleet control levels, supervised plan/budget decisions and local settings. - Closure path: Ordinary goal/resource/permission decisions return into work, but no distinct identity-level arbitration/return is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: These paths close S1/S3 decisions or security constraints. Renaming them policy or giving a user final operational say does not establish S5. - Evidence: docs/ORCHESTRA.md; docs/SUPERVISE.md; docs/FLEET.md; docs/TEAM.md; haloharness/permissions.py; haloharness/supervise/decisions.py. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-level assessment; no live model/provider execution is claimed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f6ad0072b8a1021a3d4bb4675ec159f4d8488d4/assessments/ha/halo-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f6ad0072b8a1021a3d4bb4675ec159f4d8488d4\">9f6ad00 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T19:48:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davejj1530/openorc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenOrc</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/davejj1530/openorc/tree/85bf1c5e4c81000911d8a5a90e23dbb7cf869283\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 85bf1c5e4c81000911d8a5a90e23dbb7cf869283\">85bf1c5 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: produce software/task results with durable first-party role/action/feedback composition. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous tasks, code/tool failures, delegation outcomes and changing user direction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model members choose edits, delegated work, messages and terminal results through scoped MCP tools. - Decision owner: installed Codex/Claude/OpenCode actors explicitly assembled into first-party run/team roles; external CLI internals are not separately credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, RunHandle, task briefs, MCP scope, worktrees, snapshots, journals and settlement/recovery. - Closure path: user/task brief → provider model action → local tools/files/child result → resumed model turn → captured result and reported completion. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive work choices come from model actors; host state transitions only transport/enforce them. - Caveats: requires installed authenticated CLI; source topology is not live model quality. - Boundary reachability: desktop/core constructs provider adapters and the scoped MCP server; team instructions are injected into actual actor turns, whose tool calls mutate the same live journal and return results. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/run-launch.ts; packages/core/src/services/run-turns.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-instructions.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-assignments.ts; packages/core/src/openorc.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate shared-file and assignment interference through agent-owned claim negotiation and returned correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: independent coding members may edit the same shared file, duplicate ownership or apply incompatible user direction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: members choose file ownership/release, alternative work or directed negotiation; lead/members relay concrete corrections to affected workers. - Decision owner: model-backed team members/lead own coordination choices; journal claims and refusal are enforcement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: teamclaim, teamsay/teammessage, active claims, role boundaries, captured changed files and isolated assignment integration. - Closure path: claim conflict returns holder/file → model asks colleague or selects other work → colleague releases/revises scope → later claim/work proceeds; affected active workers receive new direction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model chooses the conflict response; a fixed refusal alone is not the credited autonomous owner. - Caveats: claims are advisory and depend on model compliance; not an OS-level write sandbox, and live effectiveness is unmeasured. - Boundary reachability: Beta team execution exposes teamclaim and chat tools to running members, with conflict text returned directly to the requesting model. - Distinct S1 units: distinct coding members/contribution actors producing changes in a common project workspace or isolated child assignments. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file ownership and incompatible concurrent edits/direction, explicitly addressed by first-party shared-workspace instructions and held-path refusal. - Attenuating coordination relation: claim before editing; on collision ask current holder in chat or choose another file; release claims and deliver scoped corrections to affected members. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: holder/conflict data and chat are included in subsequent actor turns, changing who edits which files and under which direction. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: this witness names the shared-file collision, actor-selected response and resumed work; the generic message bus and hierarchy alone are not counted. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/team-instructions.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-chat.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-delivery.ts; packages/core/src/mcp-host/team.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: parent current-control over the whole active local team execution and its operational commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active assignments may need stopping/recovery, changed direction or intervention after conflicting/failed integration. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator inspects whole-team runtime and selects scoped direction, stop, actor retry, Plan/Act implementation and retained integration recovery. - Decision owner: legitimate local desktop user owns this supervisory current-control mode; lead performs delegated work coordination; fixed scheduler does not own resource discretion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime actor/attempt/task view, ownership checks, direction/admission keys, scoped delivery, stop/retry and guarded recovery receipts. - Closure path: whole-team runtime → operator intervention/direction → coordinator fences/stops/retries or delivers changed direction → actors resume or withhold work under the returned decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: P is based on the combined team-wide view and applied intervention path, not a generic stop button or the class name TeamCoordinator. No autonomous whole-system S3 choice is established. - Caveats: scope is one local team/project execution, not all organizations or a global fleet. User must exercise the supported control; source establishes capability, not observed intervention history. - Boundary reachability: desktop orchestration RPC exposes current team runtime and guarded send/stop/retry/implementPlan/recovery methods; they act on the owned execution journal rather than an adjacent dashboard. - Whole-system current view: team conversation runtime includes pinned team revision, execution actors/attempts, retained work/results and recovery state across active member cells. - Current-control decision scope: current team commitment/direction, withholding or restarting affected actors, moving planning to implementation and applying retained integration recovery; scheduler limits only enforce the selected envelope. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/team-conversation.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-coordinator.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-attempt-recovery.ts; packages/core/src/handlers/orchestration.ts. - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independent source-backed model review challenges proposed code before user publication or corrective implementation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported completion claims and defects in proposed PR code; previous findings may remain after revision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: separate review model chooses findings and summary against raw checkout/diff; user retains posting and follow-through selection. - Decision owner: dedicated review conversation/model owns audit judgment; Git checkout/draft parser and human publication are distinct support/control. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: detached PR checkout, Plan-mode review, scoped finding/summary tools, exact-commit drafts and re-review after head change. - Closure path: raw PR code/diff → separate reviewer model findings → draft visible to operator/control; selected findings can be sent into task/team implementation and later review checks whether addressed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit judgment is model-authored, not the coder completion narrative or deterministic Git diff. Human ownership of external posting does not author the audit conclusion. - Caveats: not independent provider validation or measured detection efficacy; arbitrary team member named reviewer alone is not credited. - Boundary reachability: the shipped PR-review service creates the dedicated conversation/checkout, injects review-only instructions and exposes draft finding tools; normal task/team review selections have first-party return paths. - Claim being audited: proposed source changes are correct and prior findings have been addressed. - Ordinary reporting path: implementation conversation, task result and PR description. - Complementary access path: detached current-head source checkout plus direct diff, with subsequent reviews comparing changed commits rather than merely repeating author reports. - Independence boundary: separate review conversation in Plan mode with explicit prohibition on implementation, file changes, push and posting; isolated from the author workspace. - Who acts on findings: operator sees/selects draft findings; task/team review-send paths return selected corrections to implementation; subsequent model review checks retained findings against new code. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/pull-requests.ts; packages/core/src/services/pull-request-checkout.ts; packages/core/src/services/review.ts; packages/core/src/services/team-tasks.ts. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no outside-and-future adaptation loop into OpenOrc current capability established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new task context, project lessons, user preferences and updated software versions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: memory extraction and instruction changes retain collaboration context; updater checks releases. - Decision owner: task/Orcling agents own local content changes; operator and maintainers own externally selected capability/product evolution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: project memory/retrieval, Orcling histories/instructions, model selection and release updater. - Closure path: past work/user preference is reused or a released app is updated; no prospective environmental model develops adaptation options and returns a capability decision into current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: memory, self-instruction edits and update notification do not by themselves satisfy S4. - Caveats: does not rule out a larger organization using outputs for strategy. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/memory.ts; packages/core/src/services/orcling-briefs.ts; packages/core/src/services/orclings.ts; apps/desktop/src/main/app-updates.ts. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain a persistent Orcling behavioral identity/charter in alternative agent-owned and parent-governed modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new lasting collaboration requirements or owner dissatisfaction require changing the same actor instructions across future sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Allow mode lets the Orcling choose and save revised enduring instructions; Approve/Plan requests explicit parent authorization, and user profile editing/restoration supplies a direct parent mode. - Decision owner: Orcling model for autonomous instruction revision; local user for approved/editor/restore mode and immutable permission/identity ceilings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: versioned orclinginstructions, reason/author records, action-policy approval gate, profile editing/restoration and fresh-process identity brief. - Closure path: lasting identity/working rule → model revised charter or user-selected edit/restore → version saved → next session/process injects current instructions wherever the actor works. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the autonomous base is the local actor charter, not authority to widen host permissions; parent mode separately owns identity/permission selection and approved charter change. - Caveats: not every instruction edit is S5: the positive witness concerns persistent identity/behavioral mandate. Models remain under parent-set host permission ceilings; no global institutional S5 is claimed. - Boundary reachability: Orcling tools are mounted for the saved speaker; updateInstructions authorizes according to its effective mode and saves a version. New provider processes receive the same saved identity/current charter through orclingBrief. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the long-term companion role and enduring behavioral instructions that define how this actor works across projects and conversations. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Allow mode delegates charter revision to the actor within its immutable host ceiling; Approve/Plan and user edit/restore retain parent decision over the proposed charter/identity. - Return-to-operation path: saveInstructions/restoreInstructions append the authoritative version; orclingBrief puts current instructions into each newly started process, with an explicit next-session boundary. - Evidence: packages/core/src/services/orcling-briefs.ts; packages/core/src/services/orclings.ts; packages/core/src/services/app-actions.ts; packages/core/src/services/run-approvals.ts; packages/core/src/mcp-host/orcling.ts; docs/orclings.md. - Basis: structural -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/a4215303042bfc9da6b6d818d0713d0fe9f20001/assessments/op/openorc.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit a4215303042bfc9da6b6d818d0713d0fe9f20001\">a421530 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-27T12:53:31Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ferrule</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-27\">2026-10-27</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/maximarhipkin/ferrule/tree/24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24023deffa325405db3f260a5a03d736976fa1b0\">24023de &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute coding/assistant/scheduled outcomes through a model/tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific files, commands, service responses, tool failures and verification errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select actions, tool arguments and continuation after observations. - Decision owner: root and child model-backed agents within their assigned outcomes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, sandbox/credential proxy, verification, transcript, scheduler and budget/stuck guards. - Closure path: call → tools → observations → repeated model choice; returned results and failed check tails enter subsequent operation. - Boundary reachability: shipped run/chat/gateway and child factories construct ferrulecore::Agent and equip product tools; no external cognitive harness is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: enforcement alone cannot choose the next task-dependent action if the model is removed. - Evidence: shipped runtime; core loop entry; action/verification loop; child construction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent duplicate task effort and mutually destructive sibling workspace edits. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two children attempt the same work or modify the same parent checkout. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/claim a non-conflicting task, select worktree isolation and decide when to integrate a returned branch. - Decision owner: participating model agents, with the root owning integration into its checkout. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: atomic SQLite claims/dependency refusals, isolated worktrees and returned branch/workspace notes. - Closure path: claim refusal returns current ownership/dependency information; agents select other work or wait; worker changes remain isolated until root merge/cherry-pick. - Boundary reachability: the supplied tasks and spawnagent tools are registered for the actual running tree, not hypothetical external coordination code. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent root/worker/planner agents producing separate code/work outcomes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: duplicate task claims and concurrent code edits contaminating one another. - Attenuating coordination relation: exclusive task claim plus per-worker worktree and root-controlled integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: claim/result/branch metadata changes what work an agent can take and which changes enter subsequent parent operation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: atomic task ownership and workspace separation directly regulate identified duplicate-effort/edit collisions; the shared board alone is not the witness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents choose tasks/isolation and root integration; transactions/git merely enforce those choices. - Evidence: task choice/feedback contract; atomic conflict-aware claim; tool dispatch; isolation wiring; worktree integration boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Outside git or on worktree failure a child may share the workspace; this is reported, not claimed equally isolated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: regulate current child commitments/capacity and deployment spending on behalf of the root tree. - Disturbance / variety regulated: too many live commitments, shared token scarcity and children needing intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: root selects wait/message/stop and renewed child effort; parent selects shared caps and approved administrative intervention. - Decision owner: root agent in the base tree-control mode; legitimate self-hosting operator in the separate parent mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child status/token projection, budget warnings, stop flags/tree accounting, admin approval cards, shared Hub caps and audit/config persistence. - Closure path: root interventions change running child behaviour or stop descendants; approved parent cap changes are applied to the live Hub and subsequent runs are governed by them. - Boundary reachability: messageagent/stopagent are actual root tools; owner chat ferruleadmin and dashboard use the same first-party control APIs. No adjacent benchmark supervisor is credited. - Whole-system current view: root lists its current children with role/status/token use and reports, and receives tree budget feedback; operator sees gateway health/current turns, tasks and shared caps. - Current-control decision scope: current operational commitments and scarce tree/deployment resources, beyond merely decomposing the user&#39;s task. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root chooses intervention under current operational feedback; fixed max-depth/token checks do not own that choice. In parent mode the operator&#39;s exact approval/cap decision owns the intervention. - Evidence: current child view and intervention tools; current consumption view; tree budget feedback; parent health/current control; operator status view; live cap return; approval execution. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: A is scoped to root-tree current commitments; it is not an autonomous governor of all gateway roots or OSS governance. The P mode is independently supported, not simultaneous dual ownership.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: optionally challenge a worker/parent claim or code change through a separate verifier&#39;s raw-artifact access. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a plausible completion report may hide broken tests, incorrect code or contaminated evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: verifier chooses proof commands/code inspection and judges whether the submitted claim holds. - Decision owner: a non-forked verifier agent in a separate session; parent acts on returned findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: verifier-specific prompt/role, throwaway workspace snapshot, separate transcript, optional distinct provider and parent notice/report return. - Closure path: verifier result is delivered to the parent, which can reject the claim, redirect/restart work or inspect further through its existing message/stop/tool paths. - Boundary reachability: spawnagent role=verifier is a shipped runtime mode with role-specific placement and child construction, not a test/eval-only role. - Claim being audited: a submitted operational completion claim or code change. - Ordinary reporting path: worker final report/status to parent. - Complementary access path: verifier directly reads the copied code including uncommitted/new files and runs proof commands. - Independence boundary: separate non-forked model context and snapshot; verifier does not implement/repair production changes. Same provider is allowed but is not claimed as model independence. - Who acts on findings: the root/parent with authority to redirect or stop the relevant child and change its own next operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the verifier model owns the evidence-selection/verdict, rather than a deterministic parser or self-reported success flag. - Evidence: audit role purpose; snapshot placement; fresh child context/role; return and parent response modes; verifier workspace boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Optional claim-relative audit mode, not every verifier invocation. Forking producer context, using it as ordinary same-path QA, or choosing non-isolated shared work can invalidate this independence claim; no live audit or general reliability guarantee is asserted.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied outside-and-future adaptation process with the required supervisory coupling established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: future external provider/tool/user changes could demand capability adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped future-relevant option judgment and two-way S3 adoption loop established. - Decision owner: none established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: offline reflection/probing/playbook deltas, model discovery/testing/fallbacks, doctor and install/update/admin operations. - Closure path: learning applies lessons to later prompts and admin changes current capability; the inspected triggers are retrospective internal failures or present configuration needs, without an explicit external/prospective adaptation distinction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning between runs and autonomous self-configuration are not sufficient S4 evidence. - Evidence: retrospective learning inputs; learning implementation; contrastive pass scope; admin change scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Source-relative finding; no empirical model-quality claim.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: retain legitimate ultimate authority over the deployment&#39;s autonomy/security envelope. - Disturbance / variety regulated: whether Ferrule may act without per-change consent while protected keys and owner-only boundaries remain reserved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the operator chooses Ask me first versus Protected autonomous and controls security settings that the bot cannot alter. - Decision owner: the authenticated deployment owner via the dashboard; the model cannot grant itself this authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: page-only sandbox-mode API, confirmation/fresh-config guard, protected configuration keys, audit record, saved policy and gateway restart. - Closure path: the owner&#39;s mode choice is saved and audited; the gateway restarts with the returned sandbox/trust envelope, changing subsequent admin/tool behaviour. - Boundary reachability: dashboard sandboxmode is a first-party operating endpoint explicitly omitted from chat operation routing; owner-mode and supervised admin prompts/tools are wired alternatives. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: the system&#39;s standing autonomy/delegation and protected-secret authority boundary, rather than approval of a single task. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: deployment owner; autonomous admin operation inside the envelope does not authorize changing the envelope itself. - Return-to-operation path: confirmed owner choice → policy/config save → gateway restart → subsequent operation under chosen consent/autonomy mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may perform permitted admin work, but cannot decide its own ultimate sandbox/delegation policy. - Evidence: owner-only envelope and protected limits; actual authoritative switch and return; distinct subsequent admin behaviour; chat routes omit sandbox authority. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: Linux owner mode has platform-specific constraints. This is deployment-parent policy, not a claim of project-level governance or that ordinary approval cards alone are S5.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6/assessments/fe/ferrule.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ea30365da43e2bcde1d7bcb779bed890626be1d6\">ea30365 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"4\" data-a=\"4\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-15T21:54:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/qybaihe/mu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mu</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"4\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>4</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-15\">2026-11-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/qybaihe/mu/tree/ba0aeb2d2a6f6caa5809f7bb314642827bc11a96\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ba0aeb2d2a6f6caa5809f7bb314642827bc11a96\">ba0aeb2 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Investigate, implement and check the assigned coding task through a model-directed tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing source state, failing checks and incomplete task evidence require choices about subsequent reads, edits and verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next tool action or response from task context and returned operational results. - Decision owner: Main agent running inside the packaged coding runtime; workers own bounded delegated operational choices when spawned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model transport, typed tool executor, session log, workspace and permission checks. - Closure path: Tools return observations and edits to the model context, which informs the next action and final work report. - Boundary reachability: The packaged CLI directly starts this coding loop with its bundled tools and kernel. A configured inference provider supplies responses without serving as a separate orchestration host. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Removing the agent’s semantic next-action choice leaves the tools and permissions available without an actor choosing a coding outcome. - Evidence: coding session, agent loop, launcher. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Operator checkpoints may limit particular actions. Native model quality and output success are outside the grade.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: Attenuate interference between simultaneously active Hive investigations about the same operational question. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Independently derived, stale or contradictory conclusions would otherwise continue to guide peers’ subsequent searches. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge whether a finding materially supersedes, contradicts or supports an earlier finding and route relevant material between active investigators. - Decision owner: Bundled model-backed relation and delivery decisions in active Hive mode. Investigators own their subsequent inquiry. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Append-only note and relation store, score/policy interpretation, delivery caps, receiver history and steering messages. - Closure path: A correction or dispute reaches investigators who received the previous finding, updates their context during operation, and is reflected in the main agent’s result. - Boundary reachability: The installed extension registers Hive and supplies the decision and feedback protocol when invoked. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime transports corrections, while a configured judge chooses semantic relations from case evidence and thus which corrections enter peer contexts. - Distinct S1 units: Two or more first-party investigator sessions independently read source, run checks and report findings under assigned scopes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Evidence collected by one peer can invalidate a finding another peer is already working from. - Attenuating coordination relation: A later report is classified against prior notes, and the relation determines whether the earlier claim should be corrected or treated as disputed. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The receiving bee gets a steering message about a contradiction or replacement before further inquiry. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The algorithm recognizes semantically interfering findings and targets corrective feedback to recipients already depending on them. - Evidence: relation decision, correction and peer steering, relation folding. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: Regulate the active coding cell’s current commitment when a completed turn fails to settle the goal, a blocker appears, or the work stalls. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Multiple turns and delegated steps can consume effort while a goal remains unmet or progress stalls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Evaluate accumulated current-work evidence against the active commitment and decide whether to continue with a next step, pause for intervention, or settle the goal. An interactive owner can inspect progress and redirect or terminate the current commitment. - Decision owner: Model-based goal checker in the supported goal mode; legitimate session owner in the interactive supervisory path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Goal state, hard continuation/time caps, stop and steering integration, Board/worker status, session event persistence. - Closure path: Goal verdict changes the cell’s status or enqueues a specific next instruction. Owner interruption pauses the goal and a returned user direction can replace or resume the active work. - Boundary reachability: The installed judge registers goal evaluation and both terminal and interactive controls. These operate on the same live session and goal. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The checker’s judgment about work remaining contributes discretion beyond fixed budget enforcement. The owner independently decides whether the cell should continue its present assignment. - Whole-system current view: For this one work cell, the current goal checker receives the session goal and material recent execution evidence. The operator can inspect the live task board, goal, latest events and workers. - Current-control decision scope: Continue, change next work, pause or stop the present cell commitment. No broader inter-project resource control is inferred. - Evidence: goal implementation, goal state and resume, operator mid-run controls, Board. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This assessment treats the finite goal as the whole work cell. A routine single-turn completion check alone does not establish this current-control function.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: Independently check conflicting primary-source claims reported by existing Hive investigators when ordinary coordination leaves the dispute unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Contradictory reports could leave the main agent relying on a flawed operating account. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Determine whether either disputed source claim holds, or both hold under different conditions, by checking underlying evidence and reporting the result. - Decision owner: Fresh, separately spawned verifier agent. The judge classifies the relation of its published findings, while the verifier does the evidence check. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Timed unresolved-dispute detection, fresh worker session, bounded verifier count, source-reading and checking tools, and the Hive board. - Closure path: A verifier finding reaches peers through correction and dispute routing and appears in the board and reports returned to the main agent. Unresolved disputes remain visible. - Boundary reachability: The registered Hive implementation automatically adds a targeted verifier in the supported mode when a dispute persists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A timeout only starts the additional inquiry. The fresh verifier decides what evidence to inspect and what conclusion the evidence supports. - Claim being audited: Competing reports about the same operational source or check produced by existing investigators. - Ordinary reporting path: Original investigators report through their own notes and terminal reports. - Complementary access path: The new verifier is tasked to find the underlying command, file and line and can inspect the workspace directly. - Independence boundary: Fresh agent session distinct from the original reporters, with the conflicting statements supplied as hypotheses. The source does not establish provider diversity or an external auditor. - Who acts on findings: Corrective messages reach investigators and the main agent uses the evidence in its response. - Evidence: independent dispute verifier, report return, correction routing, main-agent request. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This is a narrow audit of disputed Hive evidence. Verifier absence after a short or unavailable run remains possible, and the patch metadata judgment is not credited as a separate audit.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Convert evidence about recurring user/project demands or environment constraints into reusable capability for future coding tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Interactive runs repeatedly encounter the same project procedures or need capabilities adapted to workspace-specific conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select which evidenced procedure becomes an installed reusable skill and when a project-specific adaptation can take effect. - Decision owner: Local user with authority over installed task capabilities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model-based procedure detection/writing, draft store, accept/reject commands, skill refresh and later session discovery. - Closure path: An accepted draft becomes a project or local skill and is advertised to the model in later interaction or session initialization. The evolve path also feeds selected workspace permission adaptations into later use. - Boundary reachability: The standard interactive extension registers procedure drafts and installs accepted skills through its own commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime detects and proposes options. The owner makes the binding decision to install a draft. Observing a command substitution or recalling a stored note is narrower evidence than autonomous ownership of this general adaptation right. - External distinction: The user’s actual recurring tasks and workspace-specific procedures, including observed environment requirements. - Future / prospective distinction: A repeatable procedure is selected for later work beyond the completed example that produced it. - Adaptation option generated: Install, reject or retain a proposed reusable skill, optionally scoped to the current project. - Path back into current capability / S3: Accepted skill is persisted and discovered by later model instructions. The agent’s available procedures therefore change. - Evidence: procedure generation, operator acceptance, capability refresh, environment feedback and adaptations. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The source establishes a feature-level adaptation loop with owner acceptance. It does not establish autonomous prospective product redesign or measured recurrent benefit.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Resolve the standing policy about what the local coding cell may do autonomously and when it must return authority to its owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The drive to complete a coding task can conflict with the local owner’s standing limits on delegated operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and revise the durable permission mode governing future local sessions, including whether delegated approval decisions go to a judge or remain with the user. - Decision owner: Legitimate local workspace/session owner in the interactive permission settings path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Owner-facing mode setting, saved defaults, session binding and permission enforcement. Child sessions can receive the parent session’s current mode. - Closure path: The owner’s selected durable mode is read into new sessions and determines their future approval ownership and action boundary. - Boundary reachability: The permission modes and persistent operator path ship and are registered by default in the assessed distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Per-action tool decisions operate inside the standing mode. The mode choice remains an operator decision and affects subsequent tasks. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Whether the local harness is constituted as an ask-first or judge-delegated coding cell and which powers that choice leaves with the workspace owner. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Local owner commissions the cell and selects its enduring authority configuration. - Return-to-operation path: Owner command or desktop setting → persistent mode and current session binding → approval disposition in future work and inherited worker modes. - Evidence: standing mode and inheritance, operator command implementation, subagent inheritance. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Scope is the local work cell’s enduring delegation policy. Individual approvals and the upstream project&#39;s governance are outside this S5 claim.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ed6879aecbdc3112cbbf87c9295259d205df9e9a/assessments/mu/mu.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ed6879aecbdc3112cbbf87c9295259d205df9e9a\">ed6879a &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
